window.OMMQ={"meta":{"lede":"The material taught since Written Exam 1: lymphatics and the three lumbar-spine weeks. Every practice question the 2026 OMM 1 Canvas course posted for these weeks: interactive-session polls and case slides, faculty practice-question handouts, pre-lab quizzes and the lumbar review package. Each lecture opens with a summary. Similar questions sit together in a shaded box. Tap an answer to see why it is right, why the others are wrong, and the slides that teach it."},"lectures":[{"id":"OMM-W11","t":"Lymphatics & Diaphragms"},{"id":"OMM-W12","t":"Functional Lumbar Spine"},{"id":"OMM-W13","t":"Lumbar Muscles (Psoas, QL, Piriformis), Trigger Points & Chapman's"},{"id":"OMM-W14","t":"Clinical Lumbar Spine"}],"newLectures":["OMM-W11","OMM-W12","OMM-W13","OMM-W14"],"groups":[{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Post-mastectomy lymphedema: sympathetic tone and plan","slides":[{"src":"sl/O064-48.jpg","cap":"Chronic hypersympathetic tone decreases lymph flow, as in post-mastectomy lymphedema","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 48"},{"src":"sl/O064-50.jpg","cap":"Table: how chronic sympathetic tone stalls lymph movement","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 50"},{"src":"sl/O082-57.jpg","cap":"Treatment plan: inlet, re-dome diaphragm, pelvic diaphragm, then lymphatic pump","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 57"}],"q":[{"id":"O064-43-1","s":"A 45-year-old female presents to your office for Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment. Her past medical history is significant for breast cancer. She had a mastectomy and has under gone chemotherapy. Physical exam reveals lymph-edema of the upper right extremity. What would you include in the treatment plan of this patient?","o":["A. Reduce hyper-sympathetic activity","B. Diagnose and treat the thoracic inlet","C. Dome the abdominal diaphragm","D. Utilize lymphatic pump techniques","E. All of the above"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"All of the above","a":"","why":"<b>E. All of the above.</b> The 2026 plan for lymphedema: <b>reduce hyper-sympathetic activity</b>, <b>treat the thoracic inlet</b>, <b>re-dome the abdominal diaphragm</b>, treat the pelvic diaphragm, then apply a <b>lymphatic pump</b>.<br>A. Reduce hyper-sympathetic activity: right on its own; chronic sympathetic tone constricts lymph vessels and slows flow.<br>B. Treat the thoracic inlet: right on its own; both lymph ducts empty there, so it is opened first.<br>C. Dome the diaphragm: right on its own; it restores the pressure gradient.<br>D. Lymphatic pumps: right on its own; they push lymph through the opened path.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":43,"f":"O064"}],"t":1},{"id":"O091-1-3","s":"A 45-year-old female with a past medical history of breast cancer presents to your office for OMT. She had a right-sided mastectomy and has been treated with chemotherapy. She currently complains of lymphedema of the right upper extremity. All of the following should be included in the treatment plan for this patient except for:","o":["A. Utilize lymphatic pump techniques","B. Remove myofascial dysfunction of the thoracic inlet","C. Dome the abdominal diaphragm","D. Increase sympathetic tone"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Increase sympathetic tone","a":"","why":"<b>D. Increase sympathetic tone</b> does not belong. A short burst of sympathetic firing speeds lymph vessel contraction, but <b>chronic hypersympathetic tone</b> keeps the vessels constricted and <b>reduces lymph flow</b>. That is the 2026 lecture's example of post-mastectomy lymphedema. The plan <b>lowers</b> sympathetic tone.<br>A. Lymphatic pump techniques: belong; they push lymph once the path is open.<br>B. Thoracic inlet myofascial dysfunction: belongs; both ducts drain there, so it is treated first.<br>C. Doming the abdominal diaphragm: belongs; it restores the pressure gradient that moves lymph.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":1,"f":"O091"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Right duct vs thoracic duct drainage","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-18.jpg","cap":"Right lymphatic duct territory shaded: right arm, right head, heart and lungs","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 18"},{"src":"sl/O064-16.jpg","cap":"Thoracic duct drains everything except right head, right arm and chest","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/O065-1.jpg","cap":"Right (minor) vs left (major) duct territories and their venous endpoints","where":"2026 week 11 Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13), page 1"}],"q":[{"id":"O064-17-1","s":"Which of the following structures would be least affected by obstruction of the thoracic duct?","o":["A. Right foot","B. Spleen","C. Left kidney","D. Right hand","E. Left ear"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right hand","a":"","why":"<b>D. Right hand.</b> The <b>right lymphatic duct</b> drains only the <b>right arm, right side of the head and neck, right chest, heart</b> and most of the lungs. Everything else drains to the <b>thoracic duct</b>. A thoracic duct block spares the right hand.<br>A. Right foot: everything below the diaphragm, both legs included, drains via the cisterna chyli to the thoracic duct.<br>B. Spleen: abdominal organ, so thoracic duct.<br>C. Left kidney: abdominal, so thoracic duct.<br>E. Left ear: the left side of the head drains to the thoracic duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":17,"f":"O064"},{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":2,"f":"O091"}],"t":1},{"id":"O065-9-1","s":"A post-doctoral researcher is studying the drainage of lymphatic fluid with radionucleotide imaging in attempts to identify the structures which drain into the left (major) duct. Results of the study will most likely reveal fluid coming from the","o":["A. myocardium","B. right eye","C. right hemicranium","D. right leg","E. right upper lobe of the lung"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"right leg","a":"","why":"<b>D. Right leg.</b> The left (major) duct is the <b>thoracic duct</b>. It drains <b>both legs</b>, the abdomen and the <b>left</b> arm, head, neck and chest. The right (minor) duct drains the right arm, right head and neck, the <b>heart</b>, and the lungs except the left upper lobe.<br>A. Myocardium: heart lymph goes mainly to the right duct.<br>B. Right eye: right side of the head, so right duct.<br>C. Right hemicranium: right half of the head, so right duct.<br>E. Right upper lobe of the lung: every lobe except the left upper lobe drains to the right duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":9,"f":"O065"}]},{"id":"O065-11-4","s":"A 40-year-old female presents with chronic chest discomfort. Structural examination reveals a severe restriction to the right thoracic inlet. Which of the following is most likely to have inadequate lymphatic homeostasis in this patient?","o":["A. cisterna chyli","B. right lower extremity","C. right lumbar lymphatic trunk","D. right upper extremity","E. small intestine and ascending colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"right upper extremity","a":"","why":"<b>D. Right upper extremity.</b> The <b>right lymphatic duct</b> empties into the right subclavian–internal jugular junction inside the <b>right</b> thoracic inlet. A right inlet restriction backs up its territory: right arm, right head and neck, heart, most of the lungs.<br>A. Cisterna chyli: start of the thoracic duct at L1–L2 (first and second lumbar vertebrae); that duct ends on the left.<br>B. Right lower extremity: both legs drain to the thoracic duct.<br>C. Right lumbar lymphatic trunk: feeds the cisterna chyli, so thoracic duct.<br>E. Small intestine and ascending colon: gut lymph goes to the cisterna chyli, so thoracic duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":11,"f":"O065"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Five diaphragms: tentorium cerebelli counts","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-25.jpg","cap":"The five diaphragms: tentorium, thoracic inlet, abdominal, pelvic, popliteal","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 25"},{"src":"sl/O082-24.jpg","cap":"Definitions of abdominal, pelvic and tentorium cerebelli diaphragms","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 24"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-11-5","s":"Which of the following structures is considered to be a physiologic diaphragm?","o":["A. broad ligament of the uterus","B. greater omentum","C. hard and soft palate","D. mediastinum","E. tentorium cerebelli"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"tentorium cerebelli","a":"","why":"<b>E. Tentorium cerebelli.</b> The 2026 lecture teaches <b>five</b> transverse diaphragms: <b>tentorium cerebelli</b> (with the diaphragma sellae), thoracic inlet, abdominal (respiratory) diaphragm, pelvic diaphragm and <b>popliteal</b>. The review handout lists only four (no popliteal); the 2026 lecture's five is what counts.<br>A. Broad ligament of the uterus: a peritoneal fold, not a diaphragm.<br>B. Greater omentum: a peritoneal apron hanging from the stomach.<br>C. Hard and soft palate: the roof of the mouth; not on the list.<br>D. Mediastinum: a vertical space between the lungs, not a transverse partition.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":11,"f":"O065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Diaphragm apertures: T8, T10, T12","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-30.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm apertures: vena cava T8, esophagus T10, aorta T12","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 30"},{"src":"sl/O082-31.jpg","cap":"Three apertures and what inspiration does at each level","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 31"}],"q":[{"id":"O064-26-1","s":"The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at which vertebral level?","o":["A. T6","B. T8","C. T10","D. T12","E. L2"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"T10","a":"","why":"<b>C. T10</b> (10th thoracic vertebra). The diaphragm has three apertures: <b>T8 inferior vena cava</b>, <b>T10 esophagus</b>, <b>T12 aorta</b> (the thoracic duct passes with it). Memory aid: \"I 8 10 Eggs At 12\".<br>A. T6: no diaphragm aperture at this level.<br>B. T8: the inferior vena cava opening, in the central tendon.<br>D. T12: the aortic hiatus, which also carries the thoracic duct.<br>E. L2 (2nd lumbar vertebra): crus attachment and cisterna chyli level, not an aperture.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":26,"f":"O064"},{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":1,"f":"O091"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Inhalation creates negative intrathoracic pressure","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-34.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm descent makes intrathoracic pressure negative, moving lymph toward the heart","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 34"},{"src":"sl/O082-20.jpg","cap":"Extrinsic vs intrinsic forces that move lymph","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 20"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-10-3","s":"Respiration causes lymphatic movement centrally by the creation of","o":["A. negative intrathoracic pressure during exhalation","B. negative intrathoracic pressure during inhalation","C. positive intrathoracic pressure during exhalation","D. positive intrathoracic pressure during inhalation","E. thoracic duct smooth muscle spasms"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"negative intrathoracic pressure during inhalation","a":"","why":"<b>B. Negative intrathoracic pressure during inhalation.</b> On inhalation the diaphragm <b>contracts and descends</b>. Chest pressure becomes more negative while abdominal pressure rises. This gradient <b>pulls lymph and venous blood toward the chest</b> and heart.<br>A. Negative pressure during exhalation: exhalation raises chest pressure; it does not pull lymph in.<br>C. Positive pressure during exhalation: higher chest pressure does not draw lymph centrally.<br>D. Positive pressure during inhalation: inhalation makes chest pressure negative, not positive.<br>E. Thoracic duct smooth muscle spasms: smooth muscle contraction is an intrinsic force, not a respiratory one.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":10,"f":"O065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Arcuate ligaments: QL lateral, psoas medial","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-36.jpg","cap":"Psoas under medial, quadratus lumborum under lateral arcuate ligament","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 36"},{"src":"sl/O082-30.jpg","cap":"Lateral arcuate ligament over quadratus lumborum; gap for psoas medially","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 30"}],"q":[{"id":"O064-32-1","s":"The Quadratus lumborum passes beneath which structure?","o":["A. the lateral arcuate ligament","B. the medial arcuate ligament","C. the median arcuate ligament","D. the central tendon","E. the deltoid ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"the lateral arcuate ligament","a":"","why":"<b>A. Lateral arcuate ligament.</b> The diaphragm's back attachments arch over two muscles: the <b>quadratus lumborum</b> (QL) runs under the <b>lateral</b> arcuate ligament; the <b>psoas</b> runs under the <b>medial</b> arcuate ligament. Both fasciae tie to the tip of rib 12.<br>B. Medial arcuate ligament: arches over the psoas.<br>C. Median arcuate ligament: joins the two crura over the aorta at T12 (12th thoracic vertebra).<br>D. Central tendon: top of the dome; no bony attachment and no muscle passes under it.<br>E. Deltoid ligament: medial ankle ligament; nothing to do with the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":32,"f":"O064"},{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":1,"f":"O091"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Sibson's fascia: makeup and both ducts","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-41.jpg","cap":"Thoracic inlet borders; Sibson's fascia; thoracic duct rises to C7","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 41"},{"src":"sl/O064-10.jpg","cap":"Sibson's fascia covers the lung apex and lines the scalenes","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/O064-37.jpg","cap":"Longus colli and Sibson's fascia at the cervicothoracic junction","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"O064-11-1","s":"Which fascia is associated with the thoracic inlet?","o":["A. Scarpa’s fascia","B. Buck’s fascia","C. Colle’s fascia","D. Sibson’s fascia","E. Tensor fascia lata"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sibson’s fascia","a":"","why":"<b>D. Sibson's fascia</b> (suprapleural membrane). It caps the lung apex at the thoracic inlet. It is made of <b>prevertebral fascia and scalene fascia</b>, and both lymph ducts pass through it.<br>A. Scarpa's fascia: membranous layer of the lower abdominal wall.<br>B. Buck's fascia: deep fascia of the penis.<br>C. Colles' fascia: superficial perineal fascia, continuous with Scarpa's.<br>E. Tensor fascia lata: a hip muscle that pulls on the iliotibial band.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":11,"f":"O064"}],"t":1},{"id":"O064-39-1","s":"Fascia from which of the following structures could directly impede lymphatic flow through the right lymphatic duct?","o":["A. Longus colli muscle","B. Right crus of abdominal diaphragm","C. Levator ani muscle","D. Pyrimidalis muscle","E. Pronator teres muscle"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Longus colli muscle","a":"","why":"<b>A. Longus colli.</b> Sibson's fascia, the fascial roof of the thoracic inlet, is built from the <b>prevertebral fascia over the longus colli</b> and the <b>scalene fascia</b>. The right lymphatic duct passes through this fascia, so a tight longus colli or scalene fascia can block it.<br>B. Right crus of the diaphragm: at L1–L3 (lumbar vertebrae), far below the right duct.<br>C. Levator ani: part of the pelvic diaphragm.<br>D. Pyramidalis: a small lower abdominal wall muscle.<br>E. Pronator teres: a forearm muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":39,"f":"O064"}]},{"id":"O065-9-2","s":"Restrictions within Sibson's fascia could produce edema in","o":["A. both lower extremities","B. head and neck structures","C. the entire body","D. the left upper and both lower extremities","E. the right upper extremity"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"the entire body","a":"","why":"<b>C. The entire body.</b> <b>Both</b> ducts pass through Sibson's fascia at the thoracic inlet. The thoracic duct climbs through it to C7 (7th cervical vertebra) before turning down to the left veins; the right duct crosses it once. A restriction here can back up lymph from <b>everywhere</b>.<br>A. Both lower extremities: only part of the thoracic duct's territory.<br>B. Head and neck structures: only part of the picture.<br>D. Left upper and both lower extremities: thoracic duct only; ignores the right duct.<br>E. Right upper extremity: right duct only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":9,"f":"O065"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Thoracic inlet release: which patient benefits","slides":[{"src":"sl/O064-6.jpg","cap":"Thoracic inlet bones; releasing it lets lymph from whole body drain","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/O066-10.jpg","cap":"Thoracic inlet release: thumbs on T1, fingers on clavicles","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-12-1","s":"Thoracic inlet myofascial release would be useful for a patient who has","o":["A. functional scoliotic curve","B. inhalation dysfunction of rib 6","C. sacroiliac restriction with a tight piriformis muscle","D. thoracolumbar extended dysfunction","E. upper respiratory infection, tight scalenes, and rib 1 dysfunction"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"upper respiratory infection, tight scalenes, and rib 1 dysfunction","a":"","why":"<b>E. Upper respiratory infection, tight scalenes, rib 1 dysfunction.</b> Thoracic inlet myofascial release works on <b>rib 1, T1 (1st thoracic vertebra), manubrium, clavicles</b> and Sibson's fascia, which includes the <b>scalene</b> fascia. Opening it lets head and neck lymph drain, so it comes first in infections.<br>A. Functional scoliotic curve: a spinal curve; treated along the spine.<br>B. Inhalation dysfunction of rib 6: treat rib 6 itself (e.g., muscle energy).<br>C. Sacroiliac restriction with tight piriformis: a pelvic problem.<br>D. Thoracolumbar extended dysfunction: thoracolumbar junction, near the abdominal diaphragm, not the inlet.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":12,"f":"O065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Popliteal diaphragm: popliteal fossa release","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-47.jpg","cap":"Popliteal diaphragm: lymph capillaries crowded by the soleus arch","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 47"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-10-4","s":"A 65-year-old diabetic presents with cellulitis of the lower extremity. Lymphatic treatment using the popliteal spread technique is applied. The primary objective of this treatment is to","o":["A. Release restrictions in the popliteal fossa","B. Decrease restrictions in the joint capsule of the knee","C. Loosen medial and lateral collateral ligaments","D. Release the gastrocnemius through effluage","E. Normalize the sympathetic tone of the popliteal artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Release restrictions in the popliteal fossa","a":"","why":"<b>A. Release restrictions in the popliteal fossa.</b> The popliteal fossa is the <b>popliteal diaphragm</b>, the fifth transverse diaphragm. Its fascia and the soleus arch can crowd the lymph vessels. Popliteal spread is a <b>direct myofascial release</b> that spreads this fascia to drain the leg.<br>B. Knee joint capsule: target of articulatory technique, not this lymphatic one.<br>C. Collateral ligaments: not involved.<br>D. Gastrocnemius effleurage: effleurage is light stroking; popliteal spread spreads fascia instead.<br>E. Popliteal artery sympathetic tone: not the goal; the target is fascia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":10,"f":"O065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Treatment order: inlet, diaphragm, then pump","slides":[{"src":"sl/O066-6.jpg","cap":"Treat central drainage sites first, then work outward to the periphery","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/O082-57.jpg","cap":"Lecture order: inlet, abdominal diaphragm, pelvic diaphragm, lymphatic pump","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 57"},{"src":"sl/O066-7.jpg","cap":"Lab sequence of lymphatic techniques","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-10-1","s":"A patient presents with a mild viral syndrome. Home symptomatic care is recommended in conjunction with osteopathic manipulative treatment. Which of the following correctly outlines the correct typical sequence for lymphatic treatment?","o":["A. thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release, thoracic inlet release, lymphatic pump","B. lymphatic pump, thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release, thoracic inlet release","C. thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release, lymphatic pump, thoracic inlet release","D. thoracic inlet release, thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release, lymphatic pump","E. thoracic inlet release, lymphatic pump, thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"thoracic inlet release, thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release, lymphatic pump","a":"","why":"<b>D. Thoracic inlet, then thoraco-abdominal diaphragm, then lymphatic pump.</b> Rule: <b>remove restrictions first, then augment flow</b>, working <b>central to peripheral</b>. Open the drain (inlet), restore the pressure gradient (diaphragm), then pump.<br>A. Diaphragm first: the inlet, where the ducts empty, must open first.<br>B. Pump first: pumping against a closed inlet just backs fluid up.<br>C. Inlet last: the drain has to be opened first.<br>E. Pump before diaphragm: free the diaphragm before you pump.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":10,"f":"O065"}]},{"id":"O065-10-2","s":"In an attempt to facilitate lymphatic return to the thorax, prior to attempting lymphatic pump techniques, the physician should","o":["A. articulate the sternal angle","B. articulate the sternocostal joints","C. measure diaphragmatic excursion","D. treat peripheral restrictions","E. treat thoracic inlet restrictions"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"treat thoracic inlet restrictions","a":"","why":"<b>E. Treat thoracic inlet restrictions.</b> The inlet is the <b>terminal drainage site</b> where both ducts enter the veins. Open it <b>first</b>, then the diaphragms, then pump.<br>A. Articulate the sternal angle: does not open the terminal drain.<br>B. Articulate the sternocostal joints: same; not the first step.<br>C. Measure diaphragmatic excursion: an assessment, not a treatment.<br>D. Treat peripheral restrictions: the periphery comes <b>last</b>; work central to peripheral.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":10,"f":"O065"}],"t":1},{"id":"O091-3-1","s":"A 65-year-old man presents to the office with a several-month history of bilateral hip pain. Physical examination reveals 2+ pitting edema in both lower extremities. Osteopathic examination reveals bilateral myofascial restriction of the pelvis. A full cardiopulmonary workup is unremarkable. The physician decides to begin treatment with lymphatic techniques by placing their hands posteriorly on the cervicothoracic junction and anteriorly on the sternoclavicular joints. The tissues are assessed in all directions, and upon identifying a restriction, the physician engages it indirectly. After approximately 20 seconds, a palpable release is felt. The physician elects to continue lymphatic techniques. The next most appropriate area to assess and treat is the","o":["A. pelvic diaphragm","B. tentorium cerebelli","C. thoracic diaphragm","D. thoracic inlet","E. tongue"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"thoracic diaphragm","a":"","why":"<b>C. Thoracic diaphragm.</b> Hands on the cervicothoracic junction and sternoclavicular joints, indirect, release in 20 seconds: that is <b>thoracic inlet myofascial release</b>, already done. The 2026 order: inlet, then <b>re-dome the thoraco-abdominal diaphragm</b>, then pelvic diaphragm, then pumps.<br>A. Pelvic diaphragm: tempting with leg edema and a tight pelvis, but it comes after the thoraco-abdominal diaphragm.<br>B. Tentorium cerebelli: cranial diaphragm; not the next step for leg edema.<br>D. Thoracic inlet: just treated.<br>E. Tongue: not one of the five diaphragms.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Source lists options in shuffled order C, E, D, A, B; options here are put in their printed letter order A-E.","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":3,"f":"O091"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Contraindication: pedal pump on infected foot","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-60.jpg","cap":"Relative contraindications: unstaged cancer, coagulopathy, abscess","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 60"},{"src":"sl/O082-61.jpg","cap":"Absolute contraindications incl. splenic pump in mononucleosis","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 61"},{"src":"sl/O066-17.jpg","cap":"Pedal pump: hands on the feet, rhythmic dorsiflexion","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"O091-2-3","s":"A 45-year-old male presents to the emergency department with a 2-week history of right foot swelling. He states that the swelling started after he found a nail in his shoe. Past medical history reveals poorly controlled type 2 diabetes. Vital signs reveal a temperature of 37.8°C (100.1°F) and a heart rate of 102/min. Physical examination reveals decreased pain and vibration sensation in both feet. A probe-to-bone test is positive. A photograph of the right foot is obtained, as shown in the exhibit. Which of the following is contraindicated in the management of this patient?","o":["A. Dalrymple pump","B. Galbreath technique","C. liver pump","D. Miller pump","E. splenic pump"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Dalrymple pump","a":"","why":"<b>A. Dalrymple (pedal) pump.</b> A positive probe-to-bone test in a diabetic foot means <b>osteomyelitis</b> of the right foot. The pedal pump grips and rhythmically pumps <b>the infected foot itself</b>. Local infection (abscess, cellulitis, osteomyelitis) rules out techniques applied at that site.<br>B. Galbreath technique: jaw technique for ear drainage; far from the infection.<br>C. Liver pump: over the right lower ribs; can aid the immune response.<br>D. Miller pump: thoracic pump on the chest; fine here.<br>E. Splenic pump: absolutely contraindicated in infectious <b>mononucleosis</b> (spleen rupture risk), not here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Stem cites an exhibit photo of the right foot (diabetic foot ulcer) that is not in the printout; the text alone is enough to answer. Source lists options in shuffled order C, D, B, E, A; options here are put in their printed letter order A-E.","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":2,"f":"O091"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Physical activity beats any pump technique","slides":[{"src":"sl/O064-52.jpg","cap":"Physical activity causes the greatest increase in lymphatic return","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 52"},{"src":"sl/O064-59.jpg","cap":"Dog study: exercise raised thoracic duct flow more than pump","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 59"}],"q":[{"id":"O064-54-1","s":"Which of the following causes the greatest increase lymphatic return?","o":["A. Dalrymple Pedal pump","B. Miller thoracic pump","C. Effleurage","D. Physical activity","E. Splenic pump"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Physical activity","a":"","why":"<b>D. Physical activity.</b> The 2026 interactive session cites thoracic duct flow in dogs: lymphatic pump raised it from 1.57 to 4.8 mL/min, but <b>exercise raised it from 1.47 to 5.81 mL/min</b>. Physical activity gives the <b>greatest increase in lymph return</b>.<br>A. Dalrymple pedal pump: raises flow, but less than exercise.<br>B. Miller thoracic pump: raises flow, but less than exercise.<br>C. Effleurage: light local stroking; small effect.<br>E. Splenic pump: mainly an immune effect; less than exercise.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":54,"f":"O064"},{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":2,"f":"O091"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Galbreath technique: otitis media","slides":[{"src":"sl/O065-6.jpg","cap":"Review handout: facial sinus pressure/Galbreath useful in sinus congestion, otitis media","where":"2026 week 11 Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13), page 6"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-11-2","s":"The Galbreath technique is useful for treating those with","o":["A. bacterial conjunctivitis","B. exudative tonsillitis","C. nasal polyposis","D. recurrent otitis media","E. trigeminal neuralgia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"recurrent otitis media","a":"","why":"<b>D. Recurrent otitis media.</b> The Galbreath technique (mandibular drainage) is a slow rhythmic <b>downward and medial pull on the mandible</b>. It repeatedly opens the <b>Eustachian tube</b> so the middle ear can drain.<br>A. Bacterial conjunctivitis: an eye infection; not a Eustachian tube problem.<br>B. Exudative tonsillitis: a throat infection; not what Galbreath targets.<br>C. Nasal polyposis: a structural growth; drainage does not remove it.<br>E. Trigeminal neuralgia: a nerve pain disorder, not fluid congestion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":11,"f":"O065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Re-doming: fingers under the costal margin","slides":[{"src":"sl/O066-12.jpg","cap":"Re-doming: fingers under costal margin, follow exhalation, resist inhalation","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-9-3","s":"A patient presents with generalized edema and requests osteopathic therapy. With the hands around the thoracic cage and underneath the costal margin, the physician holds the facial tissues during patient inhalation and exhalation. This technique is called the","o":["A. facilitated positional release","B. rib raising","C. thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release","D. thoracic inlet release","E. thoracic outlet release"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release","a":"","why":"<b>C. Thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release</b> (re-doming). Fingers <b>under the costal margin</b>, pointing up. <b>Follow the diaphragm up on exhalation, resist its descent on inhalation</b>, and repeat until it releases. This restores the dome and the pressure gradient.<br>A. Facilitated positional release: position of ease plus a compression or torsion, held a few seconds.<br>B. Rib raising: fingertips at the rib angles beside the spine, lifting up and out.<br>D. Thoracic inlet release: hands on T1 (1st thoracic vertebra) and the clavicles.<br>E. Thoracic outlet release: the same neck-base region as the inlet; hands are not under the ribs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":9,"f":"O065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Pectoral traction: anterior axillary folds","slides":[{"src":"sl/O066-13.jpg","cap":"Pectoral traction: grasp pectorals at anterior axillary folds, lean back","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-11-1","s":"Pectoral traction affects lymphatic flow because the","o":["A. anterior axillary fold is directly released","B. cisterna chyli is massaged","C. clavicles are articulated","D. posterior axillary fold is directly released","E. sternum is released using the hypothenar eminence"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"anterior axillary fold is directly released","a":"","why":"<b>A. Anterior axillary fold is directly released.</b> In pectoral traction the physician, at the head of the table, grasps the <b>pectoralis muscles at the anterior axillary folds</b> and leans back. This lifts the chest into inhalation; deep breaths then work the diaphragm and boost lymph return.<br>B. Cisterna chyli massaged: it lies deep at L1–L2 (lumbar vertebrae); not touched.<br>C. Clavicles articulated: the hands hold the pectorals, not the clavicles.<br>D. Posterior axillary fold: that is a separate upper-limb drainage technique.<br>E. Sternum released with the hypothenar eminence: describes sternal pressure, not pectoral traction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":11,"f":"O065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Miller thoracic pump: sudden release, chest recoil","slides":[{"src":"sl/O066-14.jpg","cap":"Thoracic pump: push on exhalation, sudden release on inhalation","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 14"},{"src":"sl/O082-64.jpg","cap":"1918 influenza protocol: thoracic pump to increase lymphatic drainage","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 64"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-10-5","s":"A middle aged female presents with body aches, fever, and head congestion. She is assessed as having influenza and osteopathic manipulative treatment is provided. During treatment the physician utilizes the elastic recoil of the thoracic cage to create an abrupt change in intra-thoracic pressure. What is the most appropriate name of this technique?","o":["A. redoming the diaphragm","B. indirect rib release","C. pectoral traction","D. thoracic lymphatic pump of Miller","E. pedal fascial lymphatic pump"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"thoracic lymphatic pump of Miller","a":"","why":"<b>D. Thoracic lymphatic pump of Miller.</b> Hands on the upper chest push during exhalation and hold during inhalation, then <b>let go suddenly</b>. The chest's <b>elastic recoil</b> makes an abrupt negative intrathoracic pressure that pulls lymph centrally. It was the 1918 influenza protocol.<br>A. Redoming the diaphragm: slow, fingers under the costal margin; no sudden release.<br>B. Indirect rib release: moves the rib toward ease; no pressure swing.<br>C. Pectoral traction: steady pull on the anterior axillary folds.<br>E. Pedal fascial lymphatic pump: rhythmic dorsiflexion at the feet.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":10,"f":"O065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","label":"Sinus inhibitory pressure: trigeminal foramina","slides":[{"src":"sl/O066-16.jpg","cap":"Lab sinus drainage contact points: frontal, supraorbital, infraorbital, mandibular","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-11-3","s":"A manipulative treatment that involves physician contact at the patient's supraorbital, mental, and infraorbital foramina is","o":["A. anterior cervical counterstrain","B. frontal lift","C. pterygoid fossa decongestion","D. sinus inhibitory pressure","E. vomer pump technique"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"sinus inhibitory pressure","a":"","why":"<b>D. Sinus inhibitory pressure.</b> Steady pressure on the three <b>trigeminal exit foramina</b>: supraorbital (V1, ophthalmic), infraorbital (V2, maxillary) and mental (V3, mandibular). It reflexly eases sinus congestion. The source's explanation describes stroking; that is the lab's <b>sinus drainage effleurage</b>, a different technique.<br>A. Anterior cervical counterstrain: tender points on the front of the neck.<br>B. Frontal lift: cranial technique lifting the frontal bone.<br>C. Pterygoid fossa decongestion: targets the deep pterygoid region, not the facial foramina.<br>E. Vomer pump: intraoral contact on the hard palate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":11,"f":"O065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Facet orientation: flexion–extension dominates","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-9.jpg","cap":"Sagittal lumbar facets: flexion–extension greatest, rotation least, sidebending between","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/O068-7.jpg","cap":"Superior facets concave facing medially and backward; inferior convex facing laterally","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/O069-41.jpg","cap":"BUM mnemonic: lumbar superior facets face back, up and medial","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"O069-42-1","s":"The orientation of the facets in the lumbar region most significantly permit which of the following?","o":["A. Flexion","B. Abduction","C. Sidebending","D. Rotation","E. Lateral translation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Flexion","a":"","why":"<b>A. Flexion.</b> Lumbar facets lie close to the <b>sagittal plane</b> (superior facets face back, up and medial: BUM). Joint surfaces in that plane glide forward and back, so <b>flexion–extension</b> is the biggest lumbar motion; sidebending is in between and <b>rotation is the least</b>.<br>B. Abduction: a limb motion at the hip or shoulder, not a spinal motion.<br>C. Sidebending: moderate (about 20–30°), not the main motion.<br>D. Rotation: the smallest lumbar motion (about 5–7° per side) because sagittal facets block it.<br>E. Lateral translation: a small passive glide used in testing, not a main motion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":42,"f":"O069"}],"t":1},{"id":"O080-12-1","s":"A young male presents for a routine physical. His structural examination is unremarkable. You evaluate the patient's lumbar range of motion and note the main motion to be","o":["A. flexion and extension","B. rotation","C. sidebending","D. sidebending and rotation","E. translation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"flexion and extension","a":"","why":"<b>A. Flexion and extension.</b> The lumbar facets face mostly in the <b>sagittal plane</b>, so the <b>main lumbar motion is flexion–extension</b> (flexion about 40–50°, extension 20–30°).<br>B. Rotation: the least lumbar motion; the sagittal facets lock it.<br>C. Sidebending: moderate (about 20–30°), not the main motion.<br>D. Sidebending and rotation: coupled motions that follow Fryette's laws, but both are small here.<br>E. Translation: a small accessory glide, not a main motion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":12,"f":"O080"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Ferguson's angle and the A/P curves","slides":[{"src":"sl/O069-90.jpg","cap":"Ferguson angle: sacral base line versus horizontal; bigger angle means hyperlordosis","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 90"},{"src":"sl/O068-10.jpg","cap":"Normal Ferguson angle 25–35°; larger angle raises L5–S1 shear and lordosis","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/O068-11.jpg","cap":"Lumbosacral angle drawings: normal, increased-lordosis and flattened sacral bases","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"O069-92-1","s":"A 48-year-old female presents to your office with complaints of lower back pain. The pain has been progressive over the past 2 years. Physical exam reveals an abnormality of the A/P curve that is most significant at the lower lumbar spine. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the abnormal PE finding?","o":["A. A positive hip drop test","B. Loss of range of motion during side bending","C. Ferguson's angle being increased","D. Higher iliac crest on the left due to compensatory changes","E. None of the above"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ferguson's angle being increased","a":"","why":"<b>C. Increased Ferguson's angle.</b> Ferguson's (lumbosacral) angle lies between a <b>horizontal line</b> and a line along the <b>top of the sacrum</b>. Normal is 25–35° in the lecture (the IA, interactive session, calls &gt;45° hyperlordotic). A bigger angle means lower lumbar <b>hyperlordosis</b>, an A/P (front-to-back) curve change, and more <b>shear at L5–S1</b>.<br>A. Positive hip drop test: restricted sidebending, a side-to-side finding.<br>B. Lost sidebending: also side-to-side, not an A/P curve.<br>D. Higher iliac crest: a coronal asymmetry.<br>E. None of the above: C explains it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":92,"f":"O069"}]},{"id":"O068-18-1","s":"Look at curve B how has this changed the Ferguson angle and the A/P curves of the spine? Compare this to curve C. Which structures will be compressed?","o":["A. Angle decreased, curves flattened; anterior discs and bodies compressed, posterior ligaments stretched","B. Angle increased, curves deepened; anterior discs and bodies compressed, posterior ligaments stretched","C. Angle increased, curves deepened; spinous processes and facets compressed, anterior longitudinal ligament stretched","D. Angle decreased, curves flattened; spinous processes and facets compressed, anterior longitudinal ligament stretched"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Curve B increases the Ferguson (lumbosacral) angle and exaggerates the A/P curves (more lumbar lordosis and thoracic kyphosis), whereas curve C flattens them with a smaller angle. With B's hyperlordosis the posterior elements are compressed (spinous processes and zygapophyseal/facet joints) while the anterior longitudinal ligament is stretched.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Curve B is the <b>kypholordotic</b> posture: deeper lumbar lordosis and thoracic kyphosis and a steeper sacral base, so <b>Ferguson's (lumbosacral) angle increases</b>. Curve C is the flattened posture with a smaller angle. In B's hyperlordosis the back of each segment closes: <b>spinous processes and zygapophyseal (facet) joints are compressed</b> and the <b>anterior longitudinal ligament is stretched</b>.<br>A. This describes curve C, the flattened posture.<br>B. Front compression with stretched posterior ligaments is the flexed, flat-back pattern, not lordosis.<br>D. Curve B's angle rises; a smaller angle with flat curves is curve C.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O068-18-1.jpg","in":"Lateral spine diagram with three superimposed sagittal curves: A (solid, normal), B (dashed, exaggerated lordosis/kyphosis with steeper sacral base line), C (dotted, flattened curves with flatter sacral base line); arrows mark the lumbosacral angle lines.","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":18,"f":"O068"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Segmental spinous tenderness: possible disc","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-40.jpg","cap":"Interspinous and supraspinous ligaments lie behind the disc between spinous processes","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 40"},{"src":"sl/O068-36.jpg","cap":"Multifidus triangle map: interspinous ligament over the L4–L5 disc region","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-20-1","s":"Segmental tenderness to palpation of the spinous process and interspinous ligament: a positive test would give you what information?","o":["A. Iliolumbar ligament strain at L4–L5","B. A hypertonic psoas pulling on the segment","C. Sacroiliac joint somatic dysfunction","D. Facet tropism at that level","E. A possible disc problem at that segment"],"g":["E"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Possible disc issue at that segment.","a":"","why":"<b>A. Possible disc problem.</b> The 2026 lecture: tenderness over one <b>spinous process and its interspinous ligament</b> points to a possible <b>intervertebral disc</b> issue at that segment. (Pain on percussion of a spinous process is a red flag for fracture or infection.)<br>A. Iliolumbar ligament strain: tender lateral to the spine, between the L4–L5 transverse processes and iliac crest.<br>B. Psoas: lies anterior; checked with the Thomas test.<br>C. Sacroiliac joint: tender over the sacral sulcus beside the PSIS (posterior superior iliac spine), not the midline.<br>D. Facet tropism: an anatomic facet asymmetry, not a tenderness finding.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":20,"f":"O068"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Gluteus maximus lowers the lumbosacral angle","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-27.jpg","cap":"Psoas and quadriceps increase lordosis; gluteals, hamstrings, abdominals flatten it","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/O068-11.jpg","cap":"Pelvic rotation changes the lumbosacral (Ferguson) angle","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-24-1","s":"Gluteus maximus m. Let try contracting this muscle. How will this effect the L/S angle?","o":["A. Decreases it: pulls the pelvis posteriorly and reduces lordosis","B. Increases it: pulls the upper lumbar vertebrae forward","C. No change: it acts only on the hip, not the pelvis","D. Increases it: tilts the pelvis anteriorly and deepens lordosis"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"It decreases the lumbosacral (Ferguson) angle: gluteus maximus pulls the pelvis posteriorly (posterior tilt) and reduces lumbar lordosis.","a":"","why":"<b>A. It decreases the L/S (lumbosacral, Ferguson's) angle.</b> Gluteus maximus and the hamstrings <b>pull the pelvis posteriorly</b> (posterior tilt), the sacral base levels out and the lumbar spine becomes <b>hypolordotic</b>.<br>B. Pulling the upper lumbar vertebrae forward: the psoas, which increases lordosis (think arching the back).<br>C. No change: wrong; gluteus maximus arises from the ilium and sacrum, so it moves the pelvis and the angle.<br>D. Anterior tilt with more lordosis: what a hypertonic quadriceps (rectus femoris) does.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":24,"f":"O068"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Long vs short muscles: type I vs II","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-32.jpg","cap":"Erector spinae columns span many segments: long restrictors of a group","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/O079-19.jpg","cap":"Rotatores and multifidus span one or two levels: single-segment control","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/O068-34.jpg","cap":"Short multifidi and rotatores rotate individual vertebrae","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-31-1","s":"Erector spinae group (spinalis, longissimus, iliocostalis): bilateral contraction extends the L-spine; unilateral contraction causes ipsilateral extension and side bending. What type of somatic dysfunction do these muscles cause?","o":["A. Type II group curve: three or more segments rotated into the concavity","B. Pure flexion of one segment with no rotation or sidebending","C. Type II: single flexed or extended segment, rotation and sidebending same side","D. Type I: neutral group curve, sidebending and rotation to opposite sides"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Type 1 (neutral, group-curve) somatic dysfunction.","a":"","why":"<b>A. Type I (neutral) somatic dysfunction (SD).</b> The erector spinae are <b>long muscles spanning many segments</b>. Their hypertonia pulls a <b>group</b> of vertebrae into a sidebent curve, and in neutral the group rotates the opposite way (Fryette's first principle; e.g. L1–4 NSlRr = neutral, sidebent left, rotated right).<br>A. Type II is a single segment, never a group; a group curve is type I.<br>B. Pure one-plane flexion: not the pattern of a long sidebending muscle.<br>C. Single-segment type II: from <b>short deep muscles</b> (multifidus, rotatores) that control one vertebra.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":31,"f":"O068"}]},{"id":"O079-84-1","s":"Which of the following muscles are most likely to maintain a Type II somatic dysfunction?","o":["A. Spinalis","B. Iliocostalis","C. Longissimus","D. Rotatores brevis","E. Quadratus lumborum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Rotatores brevis","a":"","why":"<b>D. Rotatores brevis.</b> Short, deep muscles (rotatores, multifidus, intertransversarii) span <b>one or two segments</b>, control single vertebrae and get innervation from the fewest spinal segments, so their hypertonia holds a <b>single-segment type II</b> (non-neutral) somatic dysfunction. Rotatores brevis cross just one level.<br>A. Spinalis, B. Iliocostalis, C. Longissimus: the three <b>erector spinae</b> columns are long, multi-segment muscles; they make <b>type I group</b> curves.<br>E. Quadratus lumborum: a long muscle from the iliac crest to rib 12 and the L1–L4 transverse processes; it sidebends a group (type I).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":84,"f":"O079"}],"t":1},{"id":"O068-33-1","s":"Multifidus and rotatores (small, deep, postural back muscles that control individual vertebrae): what type of somatic dysfunction would you see as a result of hypertonia of these muscles?","o":["A. Single-segment type II, rotation and sidebending to the same side","B. Flexion of the entire region with loss of lordosis","C. Type I group curve, sidebending and rotation to opposite sides","D. Type I curve of the whole lumbar spine with sacral torsion"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Single-segment (segmental) somatic dysfunction, i.e. type II / non-neutral dysfunction of an individual vertebra.","a":"","why":"<b>B. Single-segment (segmental) type II somatic dysfunction.</b> The 2026 lecture: <b>short, deep muscles</b> drive <b>segmental</b> dysfunction because they get innervation from the fewest spinal segments and control one vertebra. A non-neutral (flexed or extended) segment rotates and sidebends to the <b>same side</b> (Fryette's second principle).<br>B. Regional flexion: multifidus actually extends locally.<br>C. Type I group curve: from long muscles such as the erector spinae.<br>D. A whole-region curve with sacral torsion: a long-restrictor pattern, not a one-segment muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":33,"f":"O068"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Disc herniation level: L4–L5 and L5–S1","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-79.jpg","cap":"Posterolateral herniation from narrow posterior longitudinal ligament; L4–L5, L5–S1 commonest","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 79"},{"src":"sl/O068-40.jpg","cap":"Anterior longitudinal ligament broad; posterior longitudinal ligament narrow, limits flexion","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-41-1","s":"At which level are you most likely to have disc injuries?","o":["A. L4–L5 and L5–S1, where the posterior longitudinal ligament is narrowest","B. T12–L1, at the thoracolumbar junction where the curves change","C. L3–L4, at the apex of the lumbar lordosis","D. L1–L2 and L2–L3, where the posterior longitudinal ligament is widest"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"The lower lumbar discs, L4–L5 and L5–S1 (most common ruptured discs L4 and L5), where the posterior longitudinal ligament is narrowest, so herniations go posterolateral.","a":"","why":"<b>A. L4–L5 and L5–S1.</b> The <b>posterior longitudinal ligament (PLL) narrows</b> as it descends; at L4–L5 it is about half its L1 width. The <b>posterolateral</b> disc is left unsupported, so herniations go posterolateral, and <b>L4–L5 and L5–S1</b> make up about 95% of lumbar herniations. Both lie in the <b>multifidus triangle</b>.<br>B. T12–L1: a transition zone for somatic dysfunction, not the common disc level.<br>C. L3–L4: herniates far less often.<br>D. Upper lumbar levels: the ligament is widest there, so herniation is rare.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Rear-view diagram of the lumbar spine showing the posterior longitudinal ligament tapering toward L5/sacrum, with a disc cross-section showing posterolateral escape paths.","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":41,"f":"O068"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Iliolumbar ligament: spring test, groin, mimics SI","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-45.jpg","cap":"Iliolumbar syndrome: groin referral, tenderness, same-side lumbosacral/sacroiliac dysfunction","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/O069-48.jpg","cap":"Iliolumbar ligament stabilizes L5 and blends with the anterior sacroiliac ligament","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 48"},{"src":"sl/O069-46.jpg","cap":"Iliolumbar ligament from L4–L5 to iliac crest; first tender in decompensation","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-42-1","s":"Iliolumbar ligament (iliac crest just above and lateral to the PSIS to the transverse processes of L4 and L5; first ligament to be strained in a lumbosacral imbalance). How would you know the ligament is irritated and the cause of pain in your patient?","o":["A. Spring test over the ligament reproduces the tenderness","B. Thomas test shows the knee lifting off the table","C. Hip drop test shows less than 20° of iliac crest drop","D. Straight leg raise reproduces leg pain at 30–70°"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Spring test (pain reproduced on springing; the ligament is tender to palpation).","a":"","why":"<b>A. Spring test.</b> In lab you locate the ligament between the <b>L4–L5 transverse processes and the iliac crest</b> and spring it on both sides; reproduced <b>tenderness</b> marks it as the pain source. It is the <b>first ligament strained</b> in a lumbosacral imbalance.<br>B. Thomas test: the knee lifting off the table means a tight iliopsoas.<br>C. Hip drop test: screens lumbar sidebending; a drop under 20° means lumbar somatic dysfunction.<br>D. Straight leg raise: radiating pain at 30–70° means L5/S1 nerve-root irritation, as from a disc.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":42,"f":"O068"}]},{"id":"O068-42-2","s":"Iliolumbar ligament (iliac crest just above and lateral to the PSIS to the transverse processes of L4 and L5). Where does the irritation of this ligament refer pain to?","o":["A. Anteriorly into the groin on the same side","B. To the arm and shoulder, with dysesthesias","C. Down the posterior thigh and calf to the heel","D. To the lateral leg and dorsum of the foot"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Anteriorly to the groin area (on the same side).","a":"","why":"<b>A. The groin, same side.</b> The iliolumbar ligament runs from the <b>L4–L5 transverse processes</b> to the iliac crest just above and lateral to the PSIS (posterior superior iliac spine). Palpating an irritated ligament often <b>refers pain anteriorly into the ipsilateral groin</b>.<br>B. Arm and shoulder dysesthesias: the lecture's referral for latissimus dorsi.<br>C. Posterior thigh and calf to the heel: an S1 nerve-root (sciatic) pattern.<br>D. Lateral leg and dorsum of the foot: the L5 dermatome.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":42,"f":"O068"}]},{"id":"O068-42-3","s":"Iliolumbar ligament irritation: what condition will it mimic?","o":["A. L5 radiculopathy from a disc herniation","B. Sacroiliac joint somatic dysfunction or inflammation","C. Spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication","D. Cauda equina syndrome"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"SI joint somatic dysfunction or inflammation.","a":"","why":"<b>A. Sacroiliac (SI) joint somatic dysfunction or inflammation.</b> The iliolumbar ligament attaches to the iliac crest beside the PSIS (posterior superior iliac spine) and <b>blends with the anterior SI ligament</b>, so its pain sits over the SI region. The lecture pairs it with same-side lumbosacral and SI dysfunction.<br>A. L5 radiculopathy: dermatomal pain to the dorsal foot with weak big-toe extension.<br>C. Spinal stenosis: older patient, leg pain on walking or standing, relieved by bending forward.<br>D. Cauda equina syndrome: saddle anesthesia and bladder or bowel dysfunction, a surgical emergency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":42,"f":"O068"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Lumbar flexion: mostly L5–S1, plus pelvic rotation","slides":[{"src":"sl/O069-37.jpg","cap":"75% of lumbar flexion at L5–S1; pelvis rotates about the hips","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-47-1","s":"Lumbar flexion ROM: where does the majority of flexion occur? What else is happening?","o":["A. L3–L4 (60–75%), at the apex of the lumbar lordosis","B. L4–L5 (60–75%), with the pelvis held still","C. L1–L2 (60–75%), with the thoracic spine extending","D. Spread evenly from L1 to L5, with no pelvic motion","E. L5–S1 (60–75%), with the pelvis rotating about the hip joints"],"g":["E"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"At L5–S1 (about 60–75% of lumbar flexion; L4–L5 plus L5–S1 give 80–90%). To reach full flexion the pelvis also rotates proportionately about the hip joints.","a":"","why":"<b>A. L5–S1, plus pelvic rotation.</b> Lumbar flexion totals about 45–50°. <b>L5–S1 gives 60–75%</b>, L4–L5 20–25% (together 80–90%), and L1–L4 only 5–10%. To reach full flexion the <b>pelvis also rotates about the hip joints</b>, so hip or hamstring restriction limits forward bending.<br>A. L3–L4: also in the 5–10% share.<br>B. L4–L5: second largest, only 20–25%, and the pelvis does move.<br>C. L1–L2: part of the small 5–10% upper share.<br>D. Even spread: no, motion concentrates at the lumbosacral junction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":47,"f":"O068"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Hip drop test: positive side drops less","slides":[{"src":"sl/O069-58.jpg","cap":"Hip drop: crest drops 20–25°, lumbar spine sidebends away from drop side","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 58"},{"src":"sl/O068-56.jpg","cap":"Positive hip drop: under 20° or no smooth curve opposite the drop","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 56"}],"q":[{"id":"O069-59-1","s":"What are the results of the Hip Drop Test in a patient whose had the diagnosis of L1-5NSrRL?","o":["A. Positive on the right","B. Positive on the left","C. Normal","D. Patient will be unable to perform the test due to low back restriction."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Positive on the right","a":"","why":"<b>A. Positive on the right.</b> In the hip drop test the patient bends one knee; that iliac crest should drop <b>20–25°</b> and the lumbar spine should <b>sidebend away</b> from the dropped side. So a right hip drop tests <b>left sidebending</b>. L1–5 NSrRL is a neutral (type I) group <b>sidebent right</b>, rotated left, so it resists left sidebending and the right crest drops less. <b>Positive is the side that drops less.</b><br>B. A left drop tests right sidebending, which this spine does freely.<br>C. Normal: no, one direction is restricted.<br>D. Unable to perform: the test still works; it is just abnormal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":59,"f":"O069"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Type II SD: barriers opposite the freedoms","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-64.jpg","cap":"Non-neutral RrSr segment rotates into the concavity; neutral mechanics above and below","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 64"},{"src":"sl/O068-60.jpg","cap":"Right rotation: right transverse process goes posterior, spinous process swings left","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 60"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-62-1","s":"Diagram of a non-neutral RRSR vertebra (body rotated right, right transverse process posterior, spinous process deviated left). Restricted motion?","o":["A. Rotation left and sidebending left","B. Rotation left and sidebending right","C. Rotation right and sidebending right","D. Rotation right and sidebending left"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Left rotation and left sidebending (plus the opposite of its flexion/extension component); somatic dysfunction is named for its freedoms, so the restrictions are the opposite directions.","a":"","why":"<b>A. Rotation left and sidebending left.</b> Somatic dysfunction is <b>named for its freedoms</b>, the position it moves into easily. A non-neutral RRSR segment rotates and sidebends <b>right</b> freely (type II: same side), so its <b>barriers are the opposites</b>: left rotation and left sidebending, plus the opposite of its flexion or extension component.<br>C. Right rotation and right sidebending: these are the freedoms.<br>C and D. Mixed-side pairs: opposite-side coupling is type I (neutral); a non-neutral segment never does it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":62,"f":"O068"}],"t":1},{"id":"O069-117-1","s":"A 31-year-old female presents with complaints of lower back pain. The pain started yesterday when she bent forwards and to the right to pick up her 9-month-old son who was playing on the floor. Which of the following motions are most likely to be found restricted on physical examination:","o":["A. Flexion, rotation left, sidebending left","B. Flexion, rotation right, sidebending right","C. Extension, rotation left, sidebending left","D. Extension, rotation right, sidebending right","E. Neutral, rotation right, side bending left"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Extension, rotation left, sidebending left","a":"","why":"<b>C. Extension, rotation left, sidebending left.</b> She bent <b>forward and to the right</b>, so the segment stuck <b>flexed, rotated right, sidebent right</b>: a type II (non-neutral) FRSR. In type II, rotation and sidebending (SB) go to the same side. Somatic dysfunction is named for its freedoms, so the <b>restrictions are the opposites</b>.<br>A. Flexion, left rotation, left SB: the barriers of an ERSR (extended, rotated and sidebent right); flexion is free here.<br>B. Flexion, right rotation, right SB: these are her freedoms.<br>D. Extension, right rotation, right SB: right motions are free.<br>E. Neutral with opposite-side rotation: a type I group pattern, not this single-segment flexion injury.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":117,"f":"O069"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"FRS/ERS: asymmetry worse toward the barrier","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-60.jpg","cap":"Posterior transverse process marks the side of rotation; spinous process goes opposite","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/O069-40.jpg","cap":"Facets open in flexion and close in extension, the motions a stuck segment lacks","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-13-5","s":"A 25-year-old male complains of low back pain. History reveals it is worse with forward bending. Structural examination reveals the right transverse process of L2 becomes more posterior with flexion. The most appropriate nomenclature for this dysfunction is","o":["A. ERLSL","B. ERRSR","C. FRLSL","D. FRRSR","E. NRRSL"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"ERRSR","a":"","why":"<b>B. ERRSR (extended, rotated right, sidebent right).</b> The <b>right transverse process (TP) is posterior</b>, so L2 is rotated right. The asymmetry gets <b>worse with flexion</b>, so flexion is the barrier and the segment is <b>extended</b>. An asymmetry that changes with flexion or extension means type II (non-neutral), so <b>sidebending is to the same side</b> as rotation.<br>A. ERLSL: wrong side; the right TP is posterior.<br>C. FRLSL and D. FRRSR: a flexed segment worsens in extension, not flexion.<br>E. NRRSL: a neutral (type I) asymmetry does not change with flexion or extension.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":13,"f":"O080"}]},{"id":"O068-61-1","s":"Fryette's second principle example: L4 F RrSr (L4 FRSr). What changes would you palpate at the TP and SP of L4 during extension?","o":["A. TPs become level and the SP returns to the midline","B. Right TP more posterior, SP deviated to the left; asymmetry increases","C. No change in TP or SP position in any position","D. Left TP more posterior, SP deviated to the right; asymmetry increases","E. Right TP more posterior, SP deviated to the right; asymmetry increases"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"The asymmetry gets worse in extension: the right transverse process becomes more prominent (posterior) and the spinous process stays deviated to the left (it does not approximate the one above), because a flexed (FRS) segment cannot extend. In flexion the TPs become more symmetric.","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> L4 FRSr is flexed, rotated right, sidebent right (type II). Rotation is named for the <b>front of the body</b>: body right, so the <b>right TP (transverse process) is posterior</b> and the <b>SP (spinous process) points left</b>. A flexed segment <b>cannot extend</b>, so in extension the right TP gets <b>more prominent</b>; in flexion the TPs even out.<br>A. TPs level: happens in flexion, the freedom of a flexed segment.<br>C. No change: describes a neutral (type I) group.<br>D. Left TP posterior: that would be left rotation.<br>E. The SP swings opposite to the body, so it goes left, not right.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":61,"f":"O068"}]},{"id":"O069-118-1","s":"If L4 is ERRSR, which of the following would be most correct:","o":["A. L5 and L3 would also be dysfunctional","B. The side bending component is exacerbated with the patient in flexion","C. The rotational component is exacerbated with the patient in extension.","D. The spinous processes will rotate to the side of the rotational component.","E. The position of the posterior transverse process will not change with flexion nor extension."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The side bending component is exacerbated with the patient in flexion","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> L4 ERRSR (extended, rotated right, sidebent right) is a type II (non-neutral) dysfunction. Its barriers are <b>flexion</b>, left rotation and left sidebending. Moving the segment <b>toward its barrier (flexion)</b> makes its rotation and sidebending asymmetry <b>more obvious</b>; extension makes it more symmetric.<br>A. Type II is a <b>single segment</b>; the levels above and below usually show neutral mechanics.<br>C. Extension is the freedom, so the asymmetry lessens in extension.<br>D. The spinous process moves <b>opposite</b> to the body: body right, spinous process left.<br>E. The posterior TP (transverse process) does change: more prominent in flexion, less in extension.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":118,"f":"O069"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Springing findings name the dysfunction","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-60.jpg","cap":"Anterior push on a transverse process rotates the body toward the opposite side","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 60"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-13-7","s":"A 55-year-old plumber presents with chronic low back pain. Structural examination in the prone position reveals the following: • Boggy area over the paravertebral tissue at L1 right • Resistance to anterior motion over the right transverse process of L1 • Resistance to anterior motion over the spinous process of L1. The most likely diagnosis is","o":["A. L1 ERSL","B. L1 ERSR","C. L1 FRSL","D. L1 FRSR","E. L1 NRLSR"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"L1 FRSR","a":"","why":"<b>D. L1 FRSR (flexed, rotated right, sidebent right).</b> Anterior pressure on the <b>spinous process</b> induces <b>extension</b>; resistance means L1 is <b>flexed</b>. Anterior pressure on the <b>right transverse process</b> induces <b>left rotation</b>; resistance means L1 is <b>rotated right</b>. A flexed segment follows type II mechanics: <b>sidebending same side</b> as rotation. The boggy tissue is the T of TART (tissue texture, asymmetry, restriction, tenderness).<br>A. ERSL and B. ERSR: extended segments move freely into extension.<br>C. FRSL: rotated left would resist pressure on the left TP.<br>E. NRLSR: a neutral group would not resist extension.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":13,"f":"O080"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Nucleus moves away from the compressed side","slides":[{"src":"sl/O069-110.jpg","cap":"Squish and squirt: flexion squeezes the front, nucleus moves posteriorly","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 110"},{"src":"sl/O068-72.jpg","cap":"Extension compresses the back; nucleus shifts anteriorly toward the anterior annulus","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 72"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-74-1","s":"During side bending to the right the nucleus moves toward the concavity or convexity?","o":["A. Concavity","B. Convexity"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Convexity – the nucleus pulposus shifts away from the loaded, compressed side (to the left when side bending right).","a":"","why":"<b>B. Convexity.</b> The nucleus pulposus moves <b>away from the compressed side</b>, like toothpaste in a squeezed tube. Sidebending right compresses the <b>right (concave)</b> side of the disc, so the nucleus shifts <b>left, toward the convexity</b>. The same rule: flexion pushes the nucleus <b>posteriorly</b>, extension pushes it <b>anteriorly</b>.<br>A. Concavity: that side is being squeezed, so the nucleus leaves it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":74,"f":"O068"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Disc load by position: seated flexed worst","slides":[{"src":"sl/O069-111.jpg","cap":"Disc load: supine lowest, lateral recumbent, standing, seated, standing flexed, seated flexed highest","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 111"},{"src":"sl/O068-82.jpg","cap":"L3 load in newtons: twisting, sidebending, sitting and lifting compared","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"O069-113-1","s":"A 51-year-old non-binary patient presents with sensory changes in the left lower extremity due to a disc herniation. The positions that would most likely aggravate their symptoms the most are:","o":["A. Extension and sidebending to the right while sitting","B. Extension and sidebending to the right while standing","C. Flexion and rotation to the right while sitting","D. Flexion and sidebending to the left when standing","E. Left lateral recumbent","F. Right lateral recumbent"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Flexion and rotation to the right while sitting","a":"","why":"<b>C. Flexion and rotation right while sitting.</b> Disc load is highest <b>sitting and flexed</b> (about 185 kg on L3 in a 70 kg person, versus about 25 kg supine). <b>Flexion</b> squeezes the front of the disc and pushes the nucleus <b>posteriorly</b> toward the nerve root, and twisting adds load (900 versus 700 newtons standing). It is the only choice joining sitting with flexion.<br>A. Extension while sitting: extension moves the nucleus anteriorly, away from the root.<br>B. Extension standing: lower load and an anterior nucleus shift.<br>D. Flexion standing: about 150 kg, less than seated flexion.<br>E and F. Lateral recumbent: low load, about 75 kg.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":113,"f":"O069"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Hyperlordosis: facets, foramina, nerve roots","slides":[{"src":"sl/O069-40.jpg","cap":"Extension closes the foramina and approximates the facets; flexion opens them","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 40"},{"src":"sl/O068-51.jpg","cap":"Intervertebral foramen holds the nerve's sensory and motor components","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-84-1","s":"A patient is referred to as having a lordotic lumbar spine. How does this impact the facet joints?","o":["A. The facets gap open and become unloaded","B. Degenerative changes from increased facet loading","C. The facets reorient into the coronal plane","D. The facets develop tropism (left–right angle mismatch)"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Degenerative changes.","a":"","why":"<b>A. Degenerative changes.</b> Hyperlordosis holds the lumbar segments in <b>extension</b>, which <b>approximates the facet (zygapophyseal) joints</b> and shifts weight onto them. The result is <b>facet degeneration</b> (facet syndrome: arthritis-like breakdown of the joint cartilage).<br>A. Gapping and unloading: happen in flexion, not lordosis.<br>C. Coronal reorientation: facet orientation is fixed anatomy; lumbar facets stay near the sagittal plane.<br>D. Facet tropism: a developmental mismatch of left and right facet angles, the most common lumbar anomaly; posture does not cause it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":84,"f":"O068"}],"t":1},{"id":"O068-84-2","s":"A patient is referred to as having a lordotic lumbar spine. How does this impact the intervertebral foramen?","o":["A. Widening","B. Widening on the concave side only","C. Narrowing","D. No change; only the central canal is affected"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Narrowing.","a":"","why":"<b>A. Narrowing.</b> Extension brings the pedicles and facets of neighboring vertebrae together and <b>closes the intervertebral foramina</b>; flexion opens them. A hyperlordotic spine sits in extension, so its foramina are <b>narrowed</b>.<br>A. Widening: happens with flexion, which is why flexion exercises ease foraminal and stenosis pain.<br>B. Widening on the concave side: sidebending narrows the foramina on the concave side; it never widens them.<br>D. No change: wrong; foraminal size changes with every flexion–extension movement.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":84,"f":"O068"}]},{"id":"O068-84-3","s":"A patient is referred to as having a lordotic lumbar spine. How does this impact the nerve roots and their sheaths?","o":["A. Protected, because the foramina widen","B. Stretched over a posterior disc bulge","C. Unaffected, because the roots exit above the disc","D. More prone to irritation"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"More prone to irritation.","a":"","why":"<b>A. More prone to irritation.</b> Each nerve root and its sheath pass through an intervertebral foramen. In hyperlordosis the foramina <b>narrow</b> and the loaded facets crowd them, so the roots are <b>more easily irritated</b>.<br>A. Protected by wider foramina: lordosis narrows the foramina, it does not widen them.<br>B. Stretched over a posterior disc bulge: the flexed, flat-back problem, where the nucleus moves posteriorly.<br>C. Unaffected: exiting above the disc does not protect a root from a narrowed foramen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":84,"f":"O068"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","label":"Lumbar HVLA: posterior transverse process up","slides":[{"src":"sl/O071-3.jpg","cap":"Lab technique list: lumbar HVLA, DiGiovanna Figure 54-1, posterior transverse process up","where":"2026 week 12 Lab Lumbar Spine Diagnosis & HVLA, slide 3"},{"src":"sl/O068-60.jpg","cap":"The posterior transverse process is on the side the vertebra rotates toward","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 60"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-3-8","s":"With the HVLA set up in the figure below, where is the posterior transverse process?","o":["A. Cephalad","B. Caudad","C. Left","D. Right"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right","a":"","why":"<b>D. Right.</b> The 2026 lab sets up lumbar HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude) with the <b>posterior transverse process (TP) up</b> (DiGiovanna text, Figure 54-1). Here the patient lies on the <b>left side</b>, right side up, so the posterior TP, the side the vertebra is rotated toward, is on the <b>right</b>.<br>A. Cephalad and B. Caudad: directions along the spine, not the side a TP is on.<br>C. Left: the down side, against the table.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O077-3-8.jpg","in":"Photo of the lumbar HVLA (lumbar roll) set-up: patient lateral recumbent on the left side with the right side up, top leg flexed; physician stands in front leaning over, one forearm on the shoulder and one on the pelvis. Asks which side the posterior TP is on (the side that is up).","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":3,"f":"O077"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Diaphragm innervation: phrenic C3–C5","slides":[{"src":"sl/O043-34.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm: main inspiratory muscle, phrenic nerve from C3–C5 rami","where":"2026 week 7 Lecture 7 Clinical Ribs, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"O074-4-2","s":"What is the innervation of the diaphragm?","o":["A. Intercostals T5–T11 + subcostal T12: motor; phrenic: peripheral sensory only","B. Phrenic: motor + central sensory; intercostals T5–T11 + subcostal T12: peripheral sensory","C. Vagus: motor; phrenic: all sensory","D. Lumbar plexus L1–L3 (via the crura): motor; phrenic: sensory","E. Phrenic: motor; vagus: all sensory"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Phrenic nerve (motor and central sensory); the peripheral diaphragm sends sensory fibers via the intercostals T5–T11 and subcostal T12.","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> The <b>phrenic nerve</b> is the only motor nerve to the diaphragm, and it also carries sensation from the <b>central</b> part. The <b>peripheral rim</b> sends sensation through the lower <b>intercostal nerves (T5–T11)</b> and the <b>subcostal nerve (T12)</b>.<br>A. Reversed: the intercostals give the diaphragm no motor supply; they carry only rim sensation.<br>C. The vagus passes through the diaphragm (esophageal hiatus) but does not move it.<br>D. The crura anchor on L1–L3, but lumbar nerves do not supply the diaphragm.<br>E. Diaphragm sensation returns by phrenic and intercostal nerves, not the vagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas","sl":4,"f":"O074"}]},{"id":"O074-4-3","s":"Where do the nerves (phrenic) exit?","o":["A. C1–C3","B. T5–T11","C. C5–C7","D. C3–C5","E. L1–L3"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"C3–C5.","a":"","why":"<b>B. C3–C5.</b> The phrenic nerve forms from the <b>C3, C4 and C5</b> ventral rami: “<b>C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive</b>.” It runs down the neck on the anterior scalene, inside the prevertebral fascia, to the diaphragm.<br>A. C1–C3 is too high; only C3 contributes.<br>B. T5–T11 intercostals carry only sensation from the diaphragm’s rim.<br>C. C5–C7 is the long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior), a common mix-up.<br>E. L1–L3 is where the crura anchor, not where the nerve comes from.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas","sl":4,"f":"O074"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Diaphragm attachments: crura on L1–L3","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-3.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm from below: crura descending onto the upper lumbar bodies","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 3"},{"src":"sl/O045-3.jpg","cap":"Review text: diaphragm attaches xiphoid, ribs 6–12, L1–L3 bodies and discs","where":"2026 week 7 Practice Questions & Review - Thorax and Rib Cage, page 3"}],"q":[{"id":"O074-4-1","s":"Where does the diaphragm insert?","o":["A. Lower ribs and sternum; crura anchor on T10–T12","B. T12–L5 bodies and discs, via tendinous arches","C. Lower ribs and sternum; crura anchor on L1–L3","D. Lower ribs and xiphoid; crura anchor on L4–L5","E. 12th rib and the transverse processes of L1–L4"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"It attaches to the lower ribs and sternum; the crura anchor at L1, L2, L3.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> The diaphragm attaches around the <b>lower ribs</b> and the <b>sternum (xiphoid)</b>. Its <b>crura</b> anchor on the bodies of <b>L1, L2 and L3</b> (right crus to L3, left to L2). That lumbar anchor is why upper lumbar somatic dysfunction (SD) can flatten the diaphragm.<br>A. T10–T12 is too high; the crura reach the lumbar bodies.<br>B. T12–L5 bodies, discs and transverse processes are the psoas major origin.<br>D. L4–L5 is too low; the crura stop at L2–L3.<br>E. Rib 12 and the L1–L4 transverse processes are the quadratus lumborum attachments.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas","sl":4,"f":"O074"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"QL muscle energy: patient hikes hip","slides":[{"src":"sl/O069-51.jpg","cap":"QL actions: side-bending and hip hiking; rib 12 to iliac crest","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-3-9","s":"You decide to treat a left hypertonic quadratus lumborum muscle on the left with muscle energy. You have the patient on their side and apply a longitudinal stretch to the quadratus lumborum muscle. What direction do you then give to the patient for the active component of the muscle energy treatment?","o":["A. Ask the patient to push their hip posteriorly","B. Ask the patient to raise their hip towards their head","C. Ask the patient to push the top leg into your pelvis","D. Ask the patient to raise their top leg into your forearm"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ask the patient to raise their hip towards their head","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> In muscle energy (ME) you hold the muscle at its barrier while the patient gently contracts it against your equal counterforce. The longitudinal stretch separates rib 12 from the iliac crest (the barrier). The quadratus lumborum (QL) <b>hikes the hip</b>, so the patient <b>pulls the hip toward the head</b> while you hold still.<br>A. Pushing the hip back is hip extension (gluteus maximus), not QL.<br>C. Pushing the leg into your pelvis uses hip muscles; it does not bring rib 12 toward the crest.<br>D. Raising the top leg is hip abduction (gluteus medius), not QL.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":3,"f":"O077"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"QL location: rib 12 to iliac crest","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-9.jpg","cap":"QL attachments: 12th rib, L1–L4 transverse processes, posterior iliac crest","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/O074-15.jpg","cap":"Palpating the QL between rib 12 and the iliac crest","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-1-2","s":"Between what two structures can you palpate the quadratus lumborum muscle?","o":["A. Lateral edge of the sacrum and the greater trocanter","B. Transverse processes of the upper lumbar and the lesser trocanter","C. Rib 12 and the posterior aspect of the iliac crest","D. Inferior to the iliac crest and lateral thigh"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Rib 12 and the posterior aspect of the iliac crest","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> The quadratus lumborum (QL) runs from the <b>12th rib</b> and L1–L4 transverse processes to the <b>posterior iliac crest</b> and iliolumbar ligament. Palpate in the gap between rib 12 and the crest, lateral to the erector spinae; its trigger points sit at the <b>lateral edge</b>.<br>A. Lateral sacrum to greater trochanter is the piriformis.<br>B. Upper lumbar transverse processes to lesser trochanter is the psoas.<br>D. Below the iliac crest to the lateral thigh is gluteus medius / tensor fasciae latae territory.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":1,"f":"O077"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Hypertonic QL: side-bend toward, short-looking leg","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-11.jpg","cap":"Right QL hypertonicity: apparent short right leg, side-bending right","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/O078-5.jpg","cap":"QL diagnosis: lateral curve, decreased side-bending, leg traction, hip drop","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-1-3","s":"Your patient presents with complaints of right sided low back pain. On palpation you find the right Quadratus Lumborum to be hypertonic with some trigger points to the lateral edge of the muscle. Upon further examination what might you expect to find?","o":["A. Decreased side-bending to the left","B. Decreased side-bending to the right","C. Increased forward flexion","D. Increased forward extension"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased side-bending to the left","a":"","why":"<b>A.</b> A tight right quadratus lumborum (QL) pulls rib 12 and the right iliac crest together, so the lumbar spine is held <b>side-bent right</b> (a type I group curve, concave right). Bending the other way stretches the tight muscle, so <b>side-bending left is decreased</b>.<br>B. Bending right is toward the tight muscle — the free direction.<br>C. QL hypertonicity decreases (not increases) side-bending and/or flexion.<br>D. A hypertonic muscle restricts motion; it does not increase range.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":1,"f":"O077"}],"t":1},{"id":"O069-54-1","s":"A patient presents with a chief complaint of low back pain that radiates to the hip and groin. Examination reveals that the lumbar spine is side bent to the right with the right leg appearing shorter in the supine position. When you apply a gentle traction to the legs you find restriction on the right. Which muscle is hypertonic?","o":["A. Right psoas","B. Right quadratus lumborum","C. Left quadratus lumborum","D. Left piriformis","E. Right hemi-diaphragm"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right quadratus lumborum","a":"","why":"<b>B. Right QL.</b> A hypertonic quadratus lumborum (QL) side-bends the lumbar spine <b>toward itself</b> and <b>hikes the innominate</b> on that side, so the right leg <b>looks short</b> (functional, not true). Gentle leg traction in supine is <b>restricted on the tight side</b>. QL trigger points refer to the hip and buttock.<br>A. Right psoas: flexed posture, positive Thomas test, anterior thigh pain; traction is not its test.<br>C. A left QL would side-bend left and make the left leg look short.<br>D. Left piriformis: buttock pain and an externally rotated leg.<br>E. The hemi-diaphragm does not hike the pelvis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":54,"f":"O069"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Psoas muscle energy: supine, leg off table","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-21.jpg","cap":"Psoas major: flexes the thigh, so its barrier is hip extension","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/O074-54.jpg","cap":"Psoas stretches all extend the hip — the barrier psoas muscle energy uses","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 54"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-2-7","s":"How do you position your patient when performing Muscle Energy for the Psoas?","o":["A. Prone with the hip in marked Flexion","B. Supine with the hip in marked Flexion","C. Prone with the effected side/leg off the table","D. Supine with the effected side/leg off the table"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Supine with the effected side/leg off the table","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> The psoas is mainly a <b>hip flexor</b>, so its barrier is <b>hip extension</b>. With the patient <b>supine</b> and the affected leg hanging <b>off the table</b>, the hip extends; the patient then lifts the knee toward the ceiling (hip flexion) against your counterforce. This is muscle energy (ME).<br>A and B. Marked hip flexion shortens the psoas — that is its ease, not its barrier.<br>C. Prone with the leg off the side drops the hip into flexion — the wrong direction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":2,"f":"O077"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Short psoas pulls lumbar into lordosis","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-31.jpg","cap":"Shortened psoas, femur fixed: lumbar extension, sacral base forward, pelvis anterior","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/O068-27.jpg","cap":"Lordosis: psoas and quadriceps increase it; hamstrings and abdominals reduce it","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"O074-30-1","s":"What happens to the lumbar spine when the psoas is shortened?","o":["A. Loses its lordosis; pelvis rotates posteriorly, as with short hamstrings","B. Pulled into extension (more lordosis); sacral base forward, pelvis rotates anteriorly","C. Pulled into extension (more lordosis); sacral base backward, pelvis rotates posteriorly","D. Unaffected, because the psoas only moves the femur","E. Side-bends away from the short psoas, convex on the psoas side"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"When the person stands the femur is fixed, so the shortened psoas pulls the lumbar spine into extension (increased lordosis) and can pull the sacral base forward / rotate the pelvis anteriorly.","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> Standing fixes the femur, so a short psoas pulls its origin (T12–L5) down and forward: the lumbar spine goes into <b>extension (increased lordosis)</b>, the <b>sacral base moves forward</b> and the <b>pelvis rotates anteriorly</b>.<br>A. Flattened lordosis with posterior pelvic tilt is the short hamstring / gluteus maximus pattern.<br>C. Half right: the psoas tips the pelvis anteriorly, not posteriorly.<br>D. The psoas attaches to the lumbar spine, so it moves it whenever the femur is fixed.<br>E. Psoas spasm side-bends the spine <b>toward</b> the tight side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas","sl":30,"f":"O074"}],"t":1},{"id":"O069-65-1","s":"Upon observation of a patient with low back pain that radiates to the anterior thigh, you find increased lumbar lordosis. In this patient, which of the following is a major cause of extension in this area of the spine?","o":["A. Hypertonic quadricep femoris muscle","B. Weakened abdominal muscles","C. Hypertonic psoas","D. Shortened hamstring muscles","E. Weakened erector spinae muscles"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypertonic psoas","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> The psoas attaches directly to the lumbar vertebrae. When it is tight and the femur is fixed in standing, it pulls the lumbar spine into <b>extension (increased lordosis)</b> — one of the major causes. Its trigger points refer to the low back and <b>anterior thigh</b>, matching this patient.<br>A. Tight quadriceps can tip the pelvis forward, but they never attach to the spine; not the major cause.<br>B. Weak abdominals allow lordosis but do not pull the spine into extension.<br>D. Short hamstrings tilt the pelvis back and flatten lordosis.<br>E. Weak erector spinae would reduce extension, not increase it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":65,"f":"O069"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Recognizing psoas syndrome: posture and exam","slides":[{"src":"sl/O078-18.jpg","cap":"Psoas syndrome: flexed, side-bent to psoas side; iliacus and contralateral piriformis","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 18"},{"src":"sl/O078-21.jpg","cap":"Findings: upper lumbar flexion dysfunction, L5 extended, ipsilateral iliacus tender point","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-13-6","s":"A young male presents with back pain. Observation reveals he is standing in a flexed, sidebent right posture. Physical examination findings include resisted hip extension on the right, a tenderpoint medial to the right ASIS. The most likely cause is","o":["A. piriformis tension on the side of the discomfort","B. S1 nerve root compression","C. sciatic nerve compression under the inguinal ligament","D. spondylolysis of the L3-L4 segments","E. hypertonic right psoas muscle"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"hypertonic right psoas muscle","a":"","why":"<b>E.</b> Psoas syndrome: <b>flexed posture side-bent toward</b> the tight psoas, <b>restricted hip extension</b> on that side, and a tender point just <b>medial to the ASIS</b> (anterior superior iliac spine; the ipsilateral iliacus point) — all on the right.<br>A. In psoas syndrome the piriformis spasm is on the <b>opposite</b> side and does not cause this posture.<br>B. S1 root compression gives neuro deficits (ankle reflex, lateral foot); psoas syndrome has a normal neuro exam.<br>C. The sciatic nerve runs through the buttock, not under the inguinal ligament.<br>D. Spondylolysis is a pars defect, usually at L5, with no tender point medial to the ASIS.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":13,"f":"O080"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Thomas test for tight iliopsoas","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-41.jpg","cap":"Positive Thomas test: extended thigh lifts off the table","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 41"},{"src":"sl/O078-19.jpg","cap":"Thomas test steps: hug opposite knee, watch gap under tested knee","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 19"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-1-1","s":"What is the name of the special test used to diagnose a hypertonic Psoas muscle?","o":["A. Thompson","B. Spurling's","C. Phalen's","D. Thomas"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Thomas","a":"","why":"<b>D. Thomas test.</b> Supine, the patient pulls one knee to the chest (flattening the lordosis). If the other thigh lifts off the table, that <b>iliopsoas</b> is tight (positive). Test both sides.<br>A. Thompson test: calf squeeze for Achilles tendon rupture.<br>B. Spurling’s test: neck compression for cervical radiculopathy.<br>C. Phalen’s test: wrist flexion for carpal tunnel syndrome.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":1,"f":"O077"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Psoas spasm from a kidney stone","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-44.jpg","cap":"Ureter runs over the psoas; passing stones cause psoas dysfunction","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 44"},{"src":"sl/O059-47.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic levels: kidney and upper ureter T10–T11; lower ureter T12–L2","where":"2026 week 9 Written Test 1 Material Review, slide 47"}],"q":[{"id":"O069-61-1","s":"Your patient with a psoas spasm has a paraspinal area at T10-T12 that is warm and boggy on the right. You suspect which of the following?","o":["A. Infection of the quadratus lumborum","B. Irritable bowel syndrome","C. Kidney stone","D. Piriformis muscle spasm","E. Poor posture and mechanics in lifting"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Kidney stone","a":"","why":"<b>C. Kidney stone.</b> Warm, boggy paraspinal tissue is an <b>acute viscerosomatic reflex</b>. The kidney and upper ureter get sympathetics from about <b>T10–T11</b> (lower ureter T12–L2), here on the right. The <b>ureter runs over the psoas</b>, so a passing stone often triggers psoas spasm.<br>A. QL (quadratus lumborum) infection is not a recognized cause of a T10–T12 visceral reflex.<br>B. Irritable bowel can upset the psoas, but it is chronic; chronic reflexes feel ropy and cool, not warm and boggy.<br>D. Piriformis spasm is somatic and sits in the buttock.<br>E. Poor lifting strains muscle; it does not make a segmental visceral reflex.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":61,"f":"O069"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Psoas syndrome: opposite piriformis and pelvic shift","slides":[{"src":"sl/O078-17.jpg","cap":"Full-blown left psoas: right pelvic shift, right piriformis spasm and pain","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/O074-37.jpg","cap":"Exam: upper lumbar flexion dysfunction, L5 extended, contralateral piriformis tender points","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"O069-73-1","s":"A 28-year-old medical student presents with a 2 week history of low back pain that radiates down his left leg just to his knee with no numbness or tingling below. He states that the low back pain began after standing up quickly. In standing observation, you find that he is bend forward over his right leg. On examination, which of the findings would you expect to find?","o":["A. L1-L5 NSlRr","B. Hypertonic piriformis on the left","C. Thomas test positive on the left","D. L5 flexed","E. Sacral rotated backwards"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypertonic piriformis on the left","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> He leans forward over his <b>right</b> leg: flexed and side-bent right = <b>right psoas</b> spasm. Psoas syndrome brings <b>contralateral piriformis</b> spasm, so the <b>left</b> piriformis is tight — matching pain down the left leg that stops at the knee.<br>A. L1–L5 NSLRR (neutral, side-bent left, rotated right) is a type I group curve; psoas syndrome gives a single non-neutral flexed somatic dysfunction at L1 or L2, side-bent right here.<br>C. The Thomas test is positive on the psoas side (right).<br>D. L5 is <b>extended</b> in psoas syndrome.<br>E. The sacrum tends to rotate forward on an oblique axis, not backward.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":73,"f":"O069"}]},{"id":"O080-14-8","s":"A 65-year-old machinist presents with chronic low back pain. History reveals he must spend several hours in a forward bent position while working. Structural examination reveals a hypertonic left psoas. The most likely associated postural screening finding is","o":["A. elevated right iliac crest","B. flexed L5 dysfunction","C. sacral shear","D. pelvic side shift right","E. posterior left sacrum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"pelvic side shift right","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> A tight <b>left</b> psoas side-bends the lumbar spine left, so the pelvis <b>shifts right</b>. The pelvic shift test, like every somatic dysfunction, is named for the side of freer motion, so it is <b>positive right</b> (contralateral).<br>A. An elevated right iliac crest is not part of psoas syndrome.<br>B. L5 is <b>extended</b>; the flexed segment is L1 or L2.<br>C. Sacral shear is not reported with psoas syndrome.<br>E. The sacrum tends to rotate forward on an oblique axis; a posterior sacrum is not the pattern.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":14,"f":"O080"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Psoas key lesion: L1–L2 flexed toward psoas","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-46.jpg","cap":"Left psoas syndrome: key non-neutral dysfunction at L1 or L2, side-bent left","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 46"},{"src":"sl/O074-37.jpg","cap":"Typical associated dysfunctions: upper lumbar (L1, L2) flexion","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-1-4","s":"Your patient presents with non-radiating left side back and hip pain. On observation you notice their posture to be slightly flexed and side-bent left. There is a positive Thomas test and negative neurologic findings. On Osteopathic Structural exam what might you expect to find?","o":["A. Group curve with concavity to the right","B. Piriformis spasm on the ipsilateral side of psoas spasm","C. L1-2 side-bent and rotated to the ipsilateral side of psoas spasm","D. Sciatic Nerve pain on the left"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L1-2 side-bent and rotated to the ipsilateral side of psoas spasm","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Flexed, side-bent left, positive Thomas test and a normal neuro exam = <b>left psoas syndrome</b>. Its key finding is a <b>non-neutral L1–L2</b> somatic dysfunction (flexed), <b>side-bent and rotated toward the psoas side</b> (left).<br>A. A group curve (type I, neutral) is not the psoas pattern; also, side-bent left means concave left.<br>B. The piriformis spasm is on the <b>contralateral</b> side.<br>D. Pain here is non-radiating with normal neuro findings; any thigh pain would be on the opposite (piriformis) side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":1,"f":"O077"}]},{"id":"O080-14-9","s":"A 65-year-old machinist presents with chronic low back pain. History reveals he must spend several hours in a forward bent position while working. Structural examination reveals a hypertonic left psoas. The most likely associated somatic dysfunction is","o":["A. L1 FRSL","B. L2 ERSR","C. L3 FRSR","D. L5 FRSL","E. posterior sacrum left"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"L1 FRSL","a":"","why":"<b>A. L1 FRSL</b> (flexed, rotated and side-bent left). The key lesion of psoas syndrome is a <b>non-neutral (type II) flexion</b> somatic dysfunction of <b>L1 or L2</b>, rotated and side-bent <b>toward the tight psoas</b>. Type II = one segment, flexed or extended, rotation and side-bending to the same side; rotation is named for the posterior transverse process.<br>B. L2 ERSR (extended, rotated and side-bent right): wrong motion and side.<br>C. L3 FRSR (flexed, rotated and side-bent right): wrong level and side.<br>D. L5 is <b>extended</b> in psoas syndrome.<br>E. A posterior left sacrum is not the reported pattern.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":14,"f":"O080"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Psoas syndrome OMT: treat L1–L2 first","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-49.jpg","cap":"Treat the key upper lumbar flexion lesion first; no thrust (HVLA) when acute","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 49"},{"src":"sl/O074-52.jpg","cap":"Passive extension stretch for flexed L1–L2","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"O074-48-1","s":"Full-blown left psoas syndrome: where to begin the OMT treatment?","o":["A. The sacral torsion on the left oblique axis","B. The key upper lumbar (L1–L2) flexion somatic dysfunction","C. The lumbosacral junction (L5–S1), with HVLA","D. The left psoas itself, with muscle energy","E. The right piriformis tender point, with counterstrain"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"The key somatic dysfunction at L1–L2 (upper lumbar flexion / non-neutral dysfunction).","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> In osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) of psoas syndrome, treat the <b>key lesion first</b>: the upper lumbar (<b>L1–L2</b>, sometimes T12 or L3) <b>flexion</b> somatic dysfunction. This alone may restore function. Acutely, use only techniques that bring the upper lumbar into <b>extension</b>.<br>A. The sacral torsion is secondary and often resolves once L1–L2 is treated.<br>C. Avoid the unstable lumbosacral area; HVLA (high-velocity low-amplitude) is not tolerated acutely.<br>D. Psoas muscle energy comes after the key lesion.<br>E. The right piriformis spasm is a downstream compensation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas","sl":48,"f":"O074"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Piriformis: external rotator; ME barrier internal rotation","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-63.jpg","cap":"Piriformis action: external rotation with straight leg; abduction at 90° flexion","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 63"},{"src":"sl/O069-72.jpg","cap":"Lateral rotator group: piriformis and five other hip external rotators","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 72"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-2-5","s":"You evaluate your patient with right sided low back and hip pain and discover a hypertonic piriformis on the right. What freedom might you expect to find when motion testing?","o":["A. External Rotation","B. Internal Rotation","C. Hip Flexion","D. Hip Extension"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"External Rotation","a":"","why":"<b>A. External rotation.</b> The piriformis is a hip <b>external rotator</b> (and an abductor when the hip is flexed 90°). A hypertonic piriformis holds the hip turned out, so external rotation (ER) is the <b>freedom</b> and internal rotation (IR) is <b>restricted</b>.<br>B. Internal rotation is the restricted direction (the barrier).<br>C. Hip flexion is mainly the iliopsoas.<br>D. Hip extension is mainly gluteus maximus and hamstrings.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":2,"f":"O077"}]},{"id":"O077-2-6","s":"When using Piriformis Muscle Energy on your patient, what position will you place your patient in to engage the barrier?","o":["A. External Rotation","B. Internal Rotation","C. Abduction","D. Hip Flexion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal Rotation","a":"","why":"<b>B. Internal rotation.</b> Muscle energy (ME) starts at the barrier — the motion that stretches the tight muscle. The piriformis externally rotates the hip, so you position into <b>internal rotation</b> (with the hip flexed, also adduction); the patient then pushes toward external rotation against you.<br>A. External rotation is the freedom — the direction the patient pushes, not the setup.<br>C. Abduction shortens the piriformis (it abducts the flexed hip).<br>D. Hip flexion alone does not engage the piriformis barrier; rotation does.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":2,"f":"O077"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Trochanteric pain: find TrP by referral pattern","slides":[{"src":"sl/O075-35.jpg","cap":"Gluteus medius trigger point 2 refers pain to the greater trochanter","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 35"},{"src":"sl/O075-33.jpg","cap":"QL trigger points refer to the buttock and greater trochanter","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 33"},{"src":"sl/O075-21.jpg","cap":"Trigger point: taut band, twitch, radiating pain; tender point: none","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"O075-36-1","s":"How would you differentiate whether trochanteric hip pain is caused by a gluteus medius, quadratus lumborum, or piriformis trigger point?","o":["A. Order an MRI to see which of the three muscles is enlarged","B. Look in each muscle for a tender point that has no taut band","C. Use referral patterns to pick muscles, then palpate for a taut band with twitch that refers to the hip","D. Inject the trochanteric bursa; if pain persists, the source is a trigger point","E. Press each muscle for a jump sign, since only trigger points give one"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Use knowledge of each muscle's trigger-point referral pattern to pick which muscles to examine, then palpate them for a taut band that gives a local twitch response and refers pain to the patient's area of complaint.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Each trigger point (TrP) has a <b>predictable referral pattern</b>; quadratus lumborum, piriformis and <b>gluteus medius TrP 2</b> TrPs can all refer to the greater trochanter. Use the patterns to pick muscles, then palpate for a <b>taut band</b> with a <b>local twitch response</b> that <b>reproduces the hip pain</b>.<br>A. Imaging has little role in finding TrPs.<br>B. A tender point has no taut band and no referral — the opposite of a TrP.<br>D. A bursa injection cannot tell the three muscles apart; bursitis shows local tenderness and inflamed tissue.<br>E. Tender points also give a jump sign, so it does not separate them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points","sl":36,"f":"O075"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Right pectoralis trigger point and SVT","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-124.jpg","cap":"Right pectoralis major trigger point between ribs 5–6 causing SVT","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 124"},{"src":"sl/O076-1.jpg","cap":"Handout: trigger points as somatovisceral reflexes; pectoralis point and SVT","where":"2026 week 13 Practice Questions & Review - Chapman's & Trigger Points (Capobianco), page 1"}],"q":[{"id":"O076-3-7","s":"An elderly male presents with shortness of breath and palpitations. An electrocardiogram is obtained and reveals a supraventricular tachyarrhythmia. Structural examination will most likely reveal which of the following?","o":["A. A crossover point at T6 left","B. flattened thoracic kyphosis involving T5-T8","C. Jones' anterior right rib 3 tenderpoint","D. Travell's right pectoralis major myofascial trigger point","E. lateral curve convex left"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Travell's right pectoralis major myofascial trigger point","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> A Travell <b>trigger point in the right pectoralis major</b>, between the <b>5th and 6th ribs</b> near the sternum, is linked to <b>supraventricular tachyarrhythmia (SVT)</b> — a somatovisceral reflex. Treating it has stopped the arrhythmia in case reports.<br>A. A crossover (where curves or fascial patterns switch direction) at T6 is not tied to SVT.<br>B. A flattened thoracic kyphosis (straight back) is linked to mitral valve prolapse, not SVT.<br>C. Jones counterstrain tender points reflect local somatic dysfunction, not this visceral reflex.<br>E. A lateral (scoliotic) curve is not an arrhythmia sign.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Chapman's & Trigger Points (Capobianco)","sl":3,"f":"O076"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Trigger point vs tender point: referral","slides":[{"src":"sl/O075-21.jpg","cap":"Trigger point refers pain when pressed; Jones tender point does not","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/O076-2.jpg","cap":"Table: Chapman's points vs trigger points vs tender points","where":"2026 week 13 Practice Questions & Review - Chapman's & Trigger Points (Capobianco), page 2"}],"q":[{"id":"O076-3-8","s":"An elderly male presents with shortness of breath and palpitations. An electrocardiogram is obtained and reveals a supraventricular tachyarrhythmia. The tissue texture abnormality discovered is different from tenderpoints because this type","o":["A. is located in specific areas of the musculoskeletal system","B. is located on the torso and never on the extremities","C. is more tender when palpated","D. results in pain perceived in a distant site called a reference zone","E. usually lacks associated sympathetic dystonia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"results in pain perceived in a distant site called a reference zone","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> A trigger point (TrP) <b>refers pain to a distant reference zone</b> when pressed; a Jones tender point is only <b>locally</b> tender. Referral is the defining difference.<br>A. Both lie in specific, predictable locations.<br>B. Both occur on the trunk and the limbs.<br>C. Both are locally tender; tender points are often exquisitely tender.<br>E. TrPs <b>do</b> cause autonomic effects (sweating, tearing, gooseflesh), so they do not lack sympathetic signs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Chapman's & Trigger Points (Capobianco)","sl":3,"f":"O076"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","label":"Stretch photo ID: hamstrings","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-76.jpg","cap":"Straight leg raise: hip flexion with straight knee loads the hamstrings","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 76"},{"src":"sl/O074-54.jpg","cap":"Contrast: psoas stretches extend the hip in a lunge","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 54"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-4-10","s":"What muscle is being stretched in the figure below?","o":["A. Rectus Abdominus","B. Quadriceps","C. Hamstrings","D. Psoas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hamstrings","a":"","why":"<b>C. Hamstrings.</b> The heel rests on the table with the <b>knee straight</b> while she <b>bends forward at the hips</b>. Hip flexion plus knee extension lengthens the hamstrings, which cross the back of both joints — the same position as a straight leg raise.<br>A. Rectus abdominis is stretched by trunk extension (arching back).<br>B. Quadriceps are stretched by knee flexion (heel to buttock) with the hip extended.<br>D. Psoas is stretched by hip extension, as in a lunge.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O077-4-10.jpg","in":"Photo of a woman standing on one leg with the other heel propped on a treatment table, knee straight, bending forward at the hips toward the raised foot (standing hamstring stretch).","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":4,"f":"O077"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Red flag: IV drug use → osteomyelitis/discitis","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-34.jpg","cap":"Red flags for spinal pathology, including IV drug use and failed 4–6 weeks","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 34"},{"src":"sl/O079-33.jpg","cap":"ACP imaging: immediate MRI for suspected spinal infection or cauda equina","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"O079-100-1","s":"28-year-old white male with back pain for 8 weeks not helped with pain medication. No trauma. History of IV drug use. Most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Vertebral osteomyelitis with discitis","B. Lumbar strain (mechanical back pain)","C. L5–S1 disc herniation","D. Isthmic spondylolisthesis","E. Lumbar spinal stenosis"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Osteomyelitis (infection, fever, erosion) and discitis","a":"","why":"<b>C. Vertebral osteomyelitis with discitis.</b> <b>IV (intravenous) drug use</b> is a red flag: bacteria in the blood seed the vertebral body and disc. Pain past <b>4–6 weeks</b> that medicine does not help is a second red flag. Order an <b>MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) with contrast</b>; it shows eroded endplates and an enhancing disc.<br>B. Lumbar strain: no red flags; it improves within weeks.<br>C. Disc herniation: dermatomal leg pain and a positive straight leg raise.<br>D. Isthmic spondylolisthesis: step-off, tight hamstrings, pain with extension.<br>E. Spinal stenosis: patients over 60; leg pain with standing and walking.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Post-contrast sagittal lumbar MRI with a green circle around an eroded, enhancing vertebral body and disc space (vertebral osteomyelitis/discitis). The vignette alone (IV drug use, 8 weeks of unrelieved pain) is enough to answer.","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":100,"f":"O079"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Cauda equina: thecal sac compression, LMN signs","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-37.jpg","cap":"Cauda equina: multiple roots compressed; bladder/bowel loss, saddle anesthesia","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/O079-114.jpg","cap":"Cauda equina history, exam and urgent surgical decompression","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 114"},{"src":"sl/O079-38.jpg","cap":"Red flags for cauda equina: saddle anesthesia, retention, bilateral sciatica","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 38"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-12-3","s":"A 55-year-old male presents to your office with low back pain radiating to his lower extremities. He states that yesterday his feet felt numb and weak, and now this feeling has progressed into his thighs. Neuromuscular examination reveals 0+ deep tendon reflexes in the lower extremities bilaterally, 3/5 muscle strength in the ankle plantar flexors, 3/5 in ankle dorsiflexors, and 3/5 in knee flexors and extensors. Digital rectal examination reveals decreased rectal tone. The most likely cause is","o":["A. a spinal cord mass effect above the level of L1","B. compression of the thecal sac","C. epidural venous plexus congestion","D. meningeal inflammation","E. severe spondylolysis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"compression of the thecal sac","a":"","why":"<b>B. Compression of the thecal sac.</b> The cord ends at <b>L1–L2</b>. Below that the thecal (dural) sac holds only nerve roots, the <b>cauda equina</b>. Squeezing many roots gives <b>lower motor neuron</b> signs: absent reflexes, flaccid weakness, saddle numbness, low rectal tone. Treat with emergency MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) and decompression within 48 hours. The source calls it \"epidural spinal cord compression\"; strictly, roots are compressed, not cord.<br>A. Cord lesion above L1: upper motor neuron signs (brisk reflexes, Babinski).<br>C. Epidural venous congestion: does not lower rectal tone.<br>D. Meningeal inflammation: fever, stiff neck, Kernig sign; no root deficits.<br>E. Spondylolysis: pars fracture; back pain without neuro deficits.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":12,"f":"O080"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Disc or foramen level → affected root","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-60.jpg","cap":"X+1 rule: L4–L5 disc compresses L5; L4 already exited","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/O079-59.jpg","cap":"L4–L5 disc presses L5 root; L5–S1 disc presses S1","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 59"},{"src":"sl/O080-1.jpg","cap":"Lumbar roots exit below their own vertebra, just above the disc","where":"2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 1"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-15-15","s":"For each numbered item (objective finding) select one heading (affected nerve root) most closely associated with it. Each lettered heading may be selected once, more than once, or not at all. An MRI of the lumbar spine reveals a posteriolateral disc herniation of the intervertebral disc of L4-L5.","o":["A. L2","B. L3","C. L4","D. L5","E. S1"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"L5","a":"","why":"<b>D. L5.</b> <b>X+1 rule</b>: a <b>posterolateral</b> herniation of the L4–L5 disc hits the root crossing that disc on its way down, <b>L5</b>. The L4 root already exited through the L4–L5 foramen, above the disc.<br>A. L2: exits at L2–L3, far above this disc.<br>B. L3: exits at L3–L4, above this disc.<br>C. L4: exits above the disc; only a far-lateral (foraminal) L4–L5 herniation hits it.<br>E. S1: hit by a posterolateral L5–S1 herniation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":15,"f":"O080"}]},{"id":"O079-118-1","s":"Disc vs. Deficit. Where is the problem?","o":["A. L4–L5 disc herniation compressing the L5 nerve root","B. L4–L5 disc herniation compressing the L4 nerve root","C. L5–S1 disc herniation compressing the L5 nerve root","D. L5 pars interarticularis fracture (spondylolysis)","E. L5–S1 disc herniation compressing the S1 nerve root"],"g":["E"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"An L5-S1 disc herniation (HNP) compressing the S1 nerve root (X+1 rule: a herniation at disc X affects nerve root X+1).","a":"","why":"<b>E.</b> The MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) shows a herniated nucleus pulposus (HNP) at <b>L5–S1</b>. <b>X+1 rule</b>: a posterolateral herniation at disc X hits root <b>X+1</b>, because root X already left through its foramen above the disc. So L5–S1 hits <b>S1</b>.<br>A. Right rule, wrong level: the image marks L5–S1, not L4–L5.<br>B. An L4–L5 disc spares L4, which has already exited.<br>C. L5 exits above the L5–S1 disc; only a far-lateral (foraminal) herniation catches it.<br>D. Spondylolysis is a pars fracture; the arrow points at the disc.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O079-118-1.jpg","in":"Sagittal lumbar MRI with L5 and S1 labeled and an arrow marking 'HNP' (herniated nucleus pulposus) bulging posteriorly at the L5-S1 disc. The level label is part of the only version of the slide.","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":118,"f":"O079"}],"t":1},{"id":"O080-15-13","s":"For each numbered item (objective finding) select one heading (affected nerve root) most closely associated with it. Each lettered heading may be selected once, more than once, or not at all. An MRI of the lumbar spine reveals severe stenosis of the intervertebral foramen affecting the nerve root between L3 and L4.","o":["A. L2","B. L3","C. L4","D. L5","E. S1"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"L3","a":"","why":"<b>B. L3.</b> Lumbar roots exit <b>below their own vertebra</b>, so the foramen between L3 and L4 carries the <b>L3 root</b>. <b>Foraminal</b> stenosis (or a far-lateral disc) squeezes this exiting root. A posterolateral L3–L4 disc would instead hit L4 (X+1 rule).<br>A. L2: exits between L2 and L3.<br>C. L4: exits between L4 and L5; at L3–L4 it is still inside the canal.<br>D. L5: exits between L5 and S1.<br>E. S1: exits through the first sacral foramen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":15,"f":"O080"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Root level from motor, reflex, sensory signs","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-117.jpg","cap":"L4, L5, S1 table: pain, numbness, weakness, screening, reflexes","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 117"},{"src":"sl/O079-61.jpg","cap":"L4: tibialis anterior, patellar reflex, medial leg sensation","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 61"},{"src":"sl/O081-7.jpg","cap":"S1: eversion, Achilles reflex, lateral foot sensation","where":"2026 week 14 Lab Clinical Lumbar, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-15-14","s":"For each numbered item (objective finding) select one heading (affected nerve root) most closely associated with it. Each lettered heading may be selected once, more than once, or not at all. Physical examination reveals an absent patellar reflex with associated sensory loss down the medial aspect of the leg.","o":["A. L2","B. L3","C. L4","D. L5","E. S1"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L4","a":"","why":"<b>C. L4.</b> L4 gives the <b>patellar reflex</b> and the skin of the <b>medial leg</b> and medial ankle. Its muscles are the quadriceps and tibialis anterior (dorsiflexion and inversion; heel-walk with the foot inverted).<br>A. L2: hip flexion and upper anterior thigh; no routine reflex.<br>B. L3: helps the patellar reflex, but its skin area stops at the medial knee.<br>D. L5: great-toe extension, dorsum of foot, no reliable reflex.<br>E. S1: Achilles reflex, lateral foot and sole, plantar flexion and eversion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":15,"f":"O080"}]},{"id":"O079-118-2","s":"Disc vs. Deficit. What are the T.A.R.T findings during your osteopathic findings? What will you find on physical exam?","o":["A. Weak quadriceps and inversion, reduced patellar reflex, numb medial leg; limited trunk flexion","B. Saddle anesthesia, low rectal tone, absent knee and ankle reflexes on both sides","C. Weak great-toe extension, no reflex change, numb dorsum of foot; limited trunk flexion","D. Weak plantar flexion and eversion, reduced Achilles reflex, numb lateral foot; limited trunk flexion","E. Brisk reflexes, upgoing Babinski toe and spastic weakness of both legs"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"S1 radiculopathy. Pain down the posterior leg to the lateral foot, numbness of the lateral foot/sole, weak plantar flexion of the great toe and foot (cannot walk on toes), weak eversion (peroneus longus), and a diminished ankle (Achilles) reflex. Osteopathic findings of root entrapment: limited trunk flexion, functional lumbar scoliosis from protective spasm, dermatomal hyperalgesia and multifidus triangle findings.","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> An L5–S1 herniation hits <b>S1</b>: weak <b>plantar flexion</b> (cannot toe-walk) and eversion, a <b>reduced Achilles (ankle) reflex</b>, and numbness of the <b>lateral foot and sole</b>. TART (tissue texture, asymmetry, restriction, tenderness) findings of root entrapment: limited trunk flexion, protective functional scoliosis, multifidus-triangle tenderness. Slide 63 gives S1 sensation as \"lateral leg and dorsum of foot\"; that is L5.<br>A. L4 root (L3–L4 disc).<br>B. Cauda equina: many roots on both sides.<br>C. L5 root (L4–L5 disc).<br>E. Upper motor neuron (cord) lesion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O079-118-2.jpg","in":"Same MRI as O079-118-1: 'HNP' at L5-S1, so the S1 root is affected.","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":118,"f":"O079"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Disc herniation: straight leg raise and Bragard","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-76.jpg","cap":"Straight leg raise steps; dorsiflexion confirms nerve root pain","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 76"},{"src":"sl/O079-75.jpg","cap":"Straight leg raise: L5/S1 root irritation, space-occupying lesion","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 75"}],"q":[{"id":"O079-118-3","s":"Disc vs. Deficit. Which special test would you consider performing on your patient?","o":["A. Babinski reflex for an upper motor neuron lesion","B. ASIS compression (pelvic rock) test for SI joint motion","C. Straight leg raise, confirmed with Bragard's test (foot dorsiflexion)","D. Thomas test for iliopsoas contracture","E. Kernig's sign for meningeal irritation"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Straight leg raise (Lasègue) test: positive if radiating leg pain is reproduced at 30-70 degrees of hip flexion. Confirm with Bragaard's test (ankle dorsiflexion). The well-leg (crossed) SLR points to a large herniation, and Valsalva/Milgram raise intrathecal pressure.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>straight leg raise (SLR)</b> stretches the sciatic roots (L4–S3). Radiating pain below the knee before about 70–80° points to a root. Lower the leg 5° and <b>dorsiflex the foot (Bragard)</b>: pain again confirms root irritation, not hamstring tightness. Pain when lifting the <b>well leg</b> (crossed SLR) means a large herniation.<br>A. Babinski: upper motor neuron lesion; a disc hits lower motor roots.<br>B. ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) compression: SI (sacroiliac) joint restriction.<br>D. Thomas test: psoas (hip flexor) contracture.<br>E. Kernig sign: meningitis, not a root test.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O079-118-3.jpg","in":"Same MRI as O079-118-1: 'HNP' at L5-S1.","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":118,"f":"O079"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Lumbar OA: ROM limited all planes, no spring","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-93.jpg","cap":"Lumbar OA: ROM restricted all planes, loss of spring, closer spinous processes","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 93"},{"src":"sl/O079-92.jpg","cap":"Lateral x-ray: thin L4–S1 discs and degenerative spurs","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 92"}],"q":[{"id":"O079-91-1","s":"Osteoarthritis: Degenerative Arthritis (Disc Degeneration). Disc Degeneration Narrowing of disc space L4/L5 &L5-S1. Osteoarthritic Spur. What would you find at this level when evaluation ROM ?","o":["A. Restricted active and passive ROM in all planes, loss of spring, abrupt end points","B. One-segment restriction that changes with flexion vs extension (Type II)","C. Restricted extension only; flexion free with a normal springy end feel","D. Full ROM; pain only on straight leg raise between 30° and 70°","E. Hypermobile segment with a step-off that persists in flexion"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Restriction of active and passive ROM in all planes, with loss of spring (rigid, abrupt end points); the spinous processes feel closer together on palpation because of loss of disc height.","a":"","why":"<b>A.</b> Worn discs at <b>L4–L5 and L5–S1</b> lose height, the facets wear and spurs form, so the segment turns stiff. <b>Active and passive ROM (range of motion) are limited in all planes</b>, the spring test is hard (<b>loss of spring</b>), and end points are rigid and abrupt. Spinous processes feel closer together.<br>B. A Type II (non-neutral) somatic dysfunction changes with flexion or extension; OA blocks every direction.<br>C. One-direction restriction with normal spring is a functional problem, not bony OA (osteoarthritis).<br>D. Isolated SLR (straight leg raise) pain: disc or root irritation.<br>E. Step-off: spondylolisthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":91,"f":"O079"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Stenosis: worse standing, relieved by flexion","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-96.jpg","cap":"Stenosis worse walking or standing, relieved sitting or bending forward","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 96"},{"src":"sl/O079-95.jpg","cap":"Stenotic canal from narrowed disc, endplate and facet osteophytes","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 95"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-13-4","s":"A 70-year-old male presents with chronic radicular low back pain associated with bilateral lower extremity pain and paresthesia. Which of the following additional findings is most closely associated with this clinical presentation?","o":["A. increased pain with standing and walking","B. extensor plantar response","C. fasciculations","D. increased deep tendon reflexes","E. symmetrical proximal hip girdle"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"increased pain with standing and walking","a":"","why":"<b>A.</b> Age 70 with bilateral radicular leg pain means <b>lumbar spinal stenosis</b>. Standing and walking hold the spine in <b>extension</b>, which narrows the canal and foramina. Sitting or leaning forward (shopping-cart sign) relieves it.<br>B. Extensor plantar response (Babinski): upper motor neuron lesion; stenosis squeezes roots.<br>C. Fasciculations: anterior horn cell disease such as ALS (amyotrophic lateral sclerosis); not a stenosis sign.<br>D. Brisk reflexes: upper motor neuron sign; root compression lowers reflexes.<br>E. Symmetric proximal hip-girdle weakness: suggests a myopathy; stenosis is usually low lumbar with distal, dermatomal signs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":13,"f":"O080"}]},{"id":"O079-97-1","s":"Spinal Stenosis: Narrowing of spinal canal. Why these patient have pain on walking and standing and flexing forward is a relieving factor?","o":["A. Standing and walking extend the spine, narrowing canal and foramina; flexion widens them","B. Standing and walking flex the spine, pushing the nucleus onto the roots; extension relieves it","C. Walking starves leg muscles of arterial blood; stopping in any posture relieves it","D. Standing raises intrathecal pressure like a Valsalva; flexing forward lowers it"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Standing and walking hold the lumbar spine in extension, which narrows the spinal canal and foramina and compresses the nerve roots (neurogenic claudication: back, buttock and thigh pain, leg weakness/heaviness). Flexing forward (sitting, leaning on a shopping cart) opens the canal and relieves symptoms.","a":"","why":"<b>A.</b> Upright posture keeps the lumbar spine in <b>extension</b>: the ligamentum flavum buckles in, the facets close, and the <b>canal and foramina narrow</b>. That causes <b>neurogenic claudication</b> (back, buttock and thigh pain, heavy legs). Leaning on a shopping cart flexes the spine and opens the space. Kemp's test (extension with rotation and sidebending to the painful side) reproduces it.<br>B. That is the disc \"squish and squirt\"; a disc worsens with flexion.<br>C. Vascular claudication: weak pulses; standing still relieves it.<br>D. Valsalva and Milgram raise intrathecal pressure to detect a disc or tumor; posture changes canal size, not this pressure.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":97,"f":"O079"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Stenosis/OA: flexion-biased exercise","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-96.jpg","cap":"Keys to osteopathic care: pelvic tilt and flexion to flatten lordosis","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 96"},{"src":"sl/O079-131.jpg","cap":"Pelvic tilt and flexion exercises vs extension-inducing exercise","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 131"}],"q":[{"id":"O079-91-2","s":"Osteoarthritis: Degenerative Arthritis (Disc Degeneration). Disc Degeneration Narrowing of disc space L4/L5 &L5-S1. Osteoarthritic Spur. Which exercise is best for this patient presentation?","o":["A. Strict bed rest until the pain has fully resolved","B. Standing extension with rotation and sidebending toward the painful side","C. Supine pelvic tilt and active resistive flexion (knees to chest) to flatten the lordosis","D. Prone extension-inducing exercises (press-ups) to increase the lordosis"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Flexion-biased exercise that flattens or decreases the lordosis: supine pelvic tilt and active resistive lumbar flexion (hips and knees flexed to the chest, pushing the knees into the hands). Avoid extension.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Lumbar OA (osteoarthritis) brings <b>spinal stenosis</b> symptoms. <b>Extension narrows</b> the canal and foramina; <b>flexion opens</b> them. The lecture's care is <b>pelvic tilt</b> plus active resistive flexion (knees to chest, push the knees into your hands) to flatten the lordosis, with OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment) for motion and blood flow.<br>A. Bed rest beyond 2 days weakens the patient and is not advised.<br>B. That is Kemp's position, a test that provokes stenosis pain.<br>D. Extension-inducing exercise is for a lost (flat) lordosis; here it worsens the narrowing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":91,"f":"O079"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Spondylo- disorders: x-ray signs","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-112.jpg","cap":"Scottie dog outlined on CT and oblique x-ray; arrows at pars defect","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 112"},{"src":"sl/O080-8.jpg","cap":"Spondylosis anterior lipping; spondylolysis collar on Scottie dog neck","where":"2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 8"},{"src":"sl/O079-98.jpg","cap":"Spondylosis MRI: endplate sclerosis, disc narrowing, osteophytes","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 98"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-15-12","s":"For each numbered item (objective finding) select one heading (diagnosis) most closely associated with it. Each lettered heading may be selected once, more than once, or not at all. A lumbar spine roentgenogram reveals a 'collar around the neck of the Scotty dog' at L5.","o":["A. spina bifida","B. spinal stenosis","C. spondylolisthesis","D. spondylolysis","E. spondylosis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"spondylolysis","a":"","why":"<b>D. Spondylolysis.</b> It is a defect (fracture) of the <b>pars interarticularis</b> without forward slip. On the <b>oblique</b> x-ray the pars is the Scottie dog's neck, and the dark break looks like a <b>collar</b>. It is most common at L5, in athletes who hyperextend.<br>A. Spina bifida: failed closure of the laminae.<br>B. Spinal stenosis: a narrowed canal, best seen on MRI (magnetic resonance imaging).<br>C. Spondylolisthesis: forward slip, seen on the lateral view.<br>E. Spondylosis: disc narrowing and osteophytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":15,"f":"O080"}]},{"id":"O079-104-2","s":"Spondylolysis (fracture of the pars interarticularis without anterior displacement of the vertebral body): Radiographic findings?","o":["A. Vertebral endplate erosion with an enhancing disc on contrast MRI","B. Disc space narrowing with anterior osteophytes (lipping) on the lateral view","C. Forward slip of L5 on S1, graded by percentage on the lateral view","D. Collar on the neck of the Scottie dog: a lucent pars defect on the oblique view"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"\"Collar on the neck of a Scottie dog\": a lucent pars defect seen on the oblique lumbar x-ray.","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> On an <b>oblique</b> lumbar x-ray the posterior elements look like a Scottie dog: <b>nose = transverse process</b>, <b>eye = pedicle</b>, ear = superior articular process, front leg = inferior articular process, <b>neck = pars interarticularis</b>. A pars fracture is a dark <b>collar</b> on the neck. The lateral view misses it because nothing has slipped.<br>A. Endplate erosion and enhancement on MRI (magnetic resonance imaging): osteomyelitis with discitis.<br>B. Narrowing and lipping: spondylosis.<br>C. Slip graded on the lateral view: spondylolisthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":104,"f":"O079"}],"t":1},{"id":"O079-102-1","s":"Spondylosis: Degenerative changes within intervertebral disc and ankylosis of adjacent vertebral bodies. Patient symptoms: Constant pain and stiffness in the low back, buttocks. Radiographic findings?","o":["A. Lucent \"collar\" on the neck of the Scottie dog on the oblique view","B. \"Bamboo spine\" with fused sacroiliac joints","C. Disc space narrowing, endplate sclerosis and osteophytes (anterior lipping)","D. Eroded vertebral endplates with an enhancing disc on contrast MRI","E. Forward slip of one vertebral body on the one below on the lateral view"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Degenerative changes: disc space narrowing, endplate sclerosis and osteophytes (anterior lipping of the vertebral bodies).","a":"","why":"<b>D. Spondylosis</b> is age-related <b>degeneration</b> of discs and vertebrae. X-ray or MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) shows <b>disc space narrowing</b>, <b>endplate sclerosis</b> and <b>osteophytes</b> (anterior lipping), sometimes bridging (ankylosis).<br>A. Scottie-dog collar: spondylo<b>lysis</b> (pars fracture).<br>B. Bamboo spine: ankylosing spondylitis, an inflammatory spondylitis, not spondylosis.<br>D. Eroded, enhancing endplates and disc: osteomyelitis with discitis.<br>E. Forward slip: spondylo<b>listhesis</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":102,"f":"O079"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Spondylo- terms: lysis, listhesis, osis","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-103.jpg","cap":"Normal pars, spondylolysis (break) and spondylolisthesis (forward slip)","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 103"},{"src":"sl/O080-10.jpg","cap":"Pars fracture with forward slip vs pars fracture without slip","where":"2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 10"},{"src":"sl/O079-98.jpg","cap":"Spondylosis is degeneration; spondylitis is inflammation","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 98"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-14-10","s":"For each numbered item (objective finding) select one heading (diagnosis) most closely associated with it. Each lettered heading may be selected once, more than once, or not at all. The anterior displacement of one vertebral body in relation to the one below is known as","o":["A. spina bifida","B. spinal stenosis","C. spondylolisthesis","D. spondylolysis","E. spondylosis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"spondylolisthesis","a":"","why":"<b>C. Spondylolisthesis.</b> \"Listhesis\" means slip: <b>forward displacement</b> of a vertebral body on the one below, most often <b>L5 on S1</b> (isthmic, from pars defects, under 50) or L4 on L5 (degenerative, over 50). It shows on the lateral x-ray and is graded I–IV by percent slip.<br>A. Spina bifida: laminar closure defect.<br>B. Spinal stenosis: narrowed canal.<br>D. Spondylolysis: pars fracture without the slip.<br>E. Spondylosis: disc degeneration with osteophytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":14,"f":"O080"}]},{"id":"O080-14-11","s":"For each numbered item (objective finding) select one heading (diagnosis) most closely associated with it. Each lettered heading may be selected once, more than once, or not at all. Degenerative changes within the intervertebral disc and ankylosing of adjacent vertebral bodies is known as","o":["A. spina bifida","B. spinal stenosis","C. spondylolisthesis","D. spondylolysis","E. spondylosis"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"spondylosis","a":"","why":"<b>E. Spondylosis.</b> General term for <b>degenerative</b> change: disc narrowing, osteophytes and stiffening (<b>ankylosis</b>) of neighbouring vertebral bodies. Patients have constant low back and buttock pain and stiffness.<br>A. Spina bifida: the laminae fail to close.<br>B. Spinal stenosis: a narrowed canal or foramen; a result of degeneration, not its name.<br>C. Spondylolisthesis: forward slip.<br>D. Spondylolysis: pars fracture with no slip.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":14,"f":"O080"}]},{"id":"O079-104-1","s":"What is spondylolysis?","o":["A. Fracture of the pars interarticularis without forward slip of the vertebral body","B. Disc degeneration with osteophytes and ankylosis of adjacent vertebral bodies","C. Failed laminar closure with the meninges herniating through the defect","D. Forward slip of a vertebral body on the one below it","E. Inflammatory arthritis of the spine and sacroiliac joints"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Fracture of the pars interarticularis without anterior displacement of the vertebral body (thought to be micro-fractures sustained over time). It usually gives rise to spondylolisthesis.","a":"","why":"<b>B. Spondylolysis</b> (\"lysis\" = break) is a fracture of the <b>pars interarticularis</b>, the bony bridge between the upper and lower facets, <b>without anterior displacement</b>. It is thought to come from repeated micro-fractures, is most common at L5 (80% bilateral), and often leads to spondylolisthesis.<br>B. Degeneration with ankylosis: spondylosis.<br>C. Meninges through a laminar defect: spina bifida meningocele.<br>D. Forward slip: spondylolisthesis.<br>E. Inflammatory arthritis: spondylitis (e.g., ankylosing spondylitis).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":104,"f":"O079"}],"t":1},{"id":"O079-105-1","s":"What is spondylolisthesis?","o":["A. Pars interarticularis fracture with the vertebral body still in line","B. Backward displacement of a vertebral body on the one below it","C. Forward displacement of a vertebral body on the one below it, most often L5 on S1","D. Disc degeneration with osteophytes and ankylosis of adjacent vertebral bodies","E. Narrowing of the spinal canal by ligamentum flavum and facet hypertrophy"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior displacement (forward slip) of a vertebral body on the one below it, most commonly L5 slipping over S1. The isthmic type comes from a pars interarticularis defect.","a":"","why":"<b>E. Spondylolisthesis</b> (\"listhesis\" = slip) is <b>anterior displacement</b> of a vertebral body on the one below, most often <b>L5 over S1</b>. The isthmic type comes from bilateral pars defects. Clues: more lordosis, flattened gluteal creases, <b>hamstring spasm</b> (the most common dysfunction), a palpable step-off.<br>A. Fracture without slip: spondylolysis.<br>B. Backward slip is retrolisthesis, not spondylolisthesis.<br>D. Degeneration with ankylosis: spondylosis.<br>E. Narrowed canal: spinal stenosis (a slip can cause it, but it is not the slip).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":105,"f":"O079"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Spondylolisthesis: conservative management","slides":[{"src":"sl/O080-9.jpg","cap":"Spondylolisthesis: 85–90% conservative, HVLA relatively contraindicated, reduce lordosis","where":"2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 9"},{"src":"sl/O079-131.jpg","cap":"Pelvic tilt and flexion exercises used to reduce lumbar lordosis","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 131"}],"q":[{"id":"O079-105-3","s":"Spondylolisthesis: How would this patient be managed?","o":["A. Prone extension (press-up) exercises to deepen the lumbar lordosis","B. HVLA thrust at the slipped segment to push the vertebra back into line","C. OMT above the slip and to the sacrum, plus pelvic-tilt exercise to reduce lordosis","D. Emergency surgical decompression within 48 hours of diagnosis"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Conservatively. Treat the somatic dysfunction above the slip and the sacral dysfunctions with OMT (HVLA is relatively contraindicated), and reduce the lumbar lordosis with exercises that tilt the pelvis and flatten the lordosis.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Most patients (<b>85–90%</b>) do well with conservative care. Treat the somatic dysfunction <b>above the slip</b> and the <b>sacral</b> dysfunctions with OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment), and <b>reduce the lordosis</b> with pelvic-tilt exercise; add weight loss and no high heels. The review package says \"avoid flexion-based exercise\"; the lecture teaches pelvic tilt, so follow the lecture.<br>A. Extension adds shear at the slip and worsens pain.<br>B. HVLA (high-velocity low-amplitude) is <b>relatively contraindicated</b> at an unstable slip.<br>D. Emergency decompression within 48 hours is for cauda equina syndrome.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":105,"f":"O079"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Spondylolisthesis: palpable shelf and its level","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-103.jpg","cap":"L5 slips forward on S1 through a pars defect","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 103"},{"src":"sl/O079-111.jpg","cap":"Pars (isthmus) fracture separates the posterior arch from the body","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 111"},{"src":"sl/O080-9.jpg","cap":"Positive step-off sign at the listhesis; grades I–IV","where":"2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 9"}],"q":[{"id":"O079-105-2","s":"Spondylolisthesis (anterior displacement of the vertebral body on the one below it; most common L5 slip over S1): What would you palpate during your examination of the Lsp? What is a palpable shelf?","o":["A. Spinous processes closer together with loss of spring at every level","B. A tender point medial to the ASIS with restricted hip extension","C. A boggy area with a step-off (shelf) between spinous processes that stays in flexion","D. Tenderness from the L4–L5 transverse processes to the iliac crest","E. A tuft of hair over a lumbosacral laminar defect"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"A boggy area with a palpable shelf (step-off sign): the spinous processes no longer line up at the slip, and the step at L5 persists even with lumbar flexion. Hamstring spasm is the most common associated dysfunction.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> In spondylolisthesis you feel <b>bogginess</b> and a <b>palpable shelf (step-off)</b>: the spinous processes no longer line up, so one sits forward of its neighbour. The shelf stays when the patient <b>flexes</b>, so it is not just posture. Expect <b>hamstring spasm</b> too.<br>A. Close spinous processes with loss of spring: disc degeneration (OA, osteoarthritis).<br>B. Tender point medial to the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine): psoas or anterior L1 counterstrain point.<br>D. L4–L5 transverse processes to iliac crest: iliolumbar ligament strain.<br>E. Hair tuft (faun tail nevus): spina bifida occulta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":105,"f":"O079"}],"t":1},{"id":"O079-110-1","s":"In B, a fracture or separation of the pars: Sliding of L5 on S1. Anterior slippage at body of L5 occurs, movement of the posterior arch of L5 is resisted by the facet surfaces of S1. The resulting shelf is palpable at which level?","o":["A. Between the L5 spinous process and the sacrum, because the L5 arch slides forward","B. Between the L4 and L5 spinous processes, because the L5 arch stays back","C. Between S1 and S2 on the median sacral crest","D. Between the L3 and L4 spinous processes, two levels above the slip"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"At the L5 spinous process, as a step-off between the L4 and L5 spinous processes (the level just above the slip). The L5 posterior arch stays back while L4 rides forward with the L5 body.","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> With a <b>pars fracture</b> the L5 body slides forward and carries L4 and everything above with it. The <b>L5 posterior arch</b> (lamina and spinous process) is held back by the S1 facets. So the L5 spinous process stays prominent and <b>L4 sits forward of it</b>: the shelf is felt between <b>L4 and L5</b>, one level above the slip.<br>A. That happens when the whole vertebra slips with an intact arch (degenerative type); here the arch is broken.<br>C. The sacrum does not move in an L5–S1 slip.<br>D. L3 and L4 move forward together, so there is no step between them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":110,"f":"O079"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","label":"Spina bifida types","slides":[{"src":"sl/O080-3.jpg","cap":"Spina bifida occulta, meningocele, meningomyelocele defined","where":"2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 3"},{"src":"sl/O079-13.jpg","cap":"Faun tail nevus and open sacral spina bifida in infants","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-12-2","s":"A newborn is delivered with a posterior lumbar mass. Ultrasound revealed that the mass appears to be composed of meningeal and neural tissue. The most likely diagnosis is","o":["A. disc herniation","B. spina bifida meningocele","C. spina bifida meningomyelocele","D. spina bifida occulta","E. spondylolysis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"spina bifida meningomyelocele","a":"","why":"<b>C. Spina bifida meningomyelocele.</b> The laminae fail to close and both the <b>meninges and neural tissue</b> (cord or roots) herniate into the sac; it usually causes neurologic deficits.<br>A. Disc herniation: goes into the canal, never out as a back mass.<br>B. Meningocele: a sac of <b>meninges only</b>, no neural tissue.<br>D. Occulta: no herniation; often only a hair tuft (faun tail nevus).<br>E. Spondylolysis: pars fracture, no mass.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":12,"f":"O080"}],"t":1}]}],"sum":{"OMM-W11":{"bp":"Lymph is a passive second circulation that relies on outside forces, above all the pressure swings made by breathing through the body's transverse diaphragms. Lymphatic osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) first opens the drains (thoracic inlet, then the diaphragms) and only then pushes fluid with pumps, working from the center out.","sec":[{"h":"Lymphatic system: jobs and parts","html":"<p>The lymphatic system is a <b>second circulatory system</b>. It is <b>passive</b>: outside forces drive much of its flow, and when it fails, <b>edema</b> follows. A.T. Still: let the lymphatics receive and discharge naturally, or retained fluid brings \"fermentation, fever, sickness and death\". </p><p>Three jobs: return <b>interstitial protein</b> to the blood; bring antigens to immune cells; and drain <b>inflammatory mediators</b>, microbes and cells so acute inflammation can resolve.</p><p>Parts: lymphoid tissue (nodes, spleen, thymus, tonsils, appendix, gut and lung tissue, liver), collecting vessels, and clear protein-rich lymph. The <b>spleen</b> is the largest lymphoid mass, on the left deep to <b>ribs 9–11</b>, below the diaphragm. It removes damaged red cells, makes antibodies and clears encapsulated bacteria. The thymus (superior mediastinum) processes T cells and involutes in adults. Gut lymphoid tissue (Peyer's patches, lacteals) sits beside the enteric nervous system, a rationale for lymphatic treatment in irritable bowel disorders. Lymph vessels reach every tissue <b>except the CNS (central nervous system), epidermis and endomysium</b>.</p><p>Development: paired jugular lymph sacs form in week 5. Lymphoid tissue grows until about <b>age 6–9</b>, then regresses to adult levels by 15–16.</p>","figs":[]},{"h":"The path of lymph and the two ducts","html":"<p>Route: blind, leaky <b>lymph capillary</b> (one endothelial layer, held open by anchoring filaments) → <b>lymphangion</b> (a vessel segment with smooth muscle and one-way valves) → trunks → nodes along the way → right lymphatic duct or thoracic duct → great veins. Lymphangions contract one after another, so lymph in the main vessels is <b>propelled by the vessel itself</b>, one way only.</p><p>Trunks from the abdomen, pelvis and legs join the <b>cisterna chyli</b>, a sac at L1–L2 (first and second lumbar vertebrae), in front of and to the right of the vertebral bodies, behind the diaphragm's crus. It starts the thoracic duct, the largest lymph vessel (about 18 inches). The duct passes the <b>aortic hiatus at T12</b> (12th thoracic vertebra), rises through Sibson's fascia to C7 (7th cervical vertebra), then turns down to the <b>left internal jugular–subclavian junction</b>. It crosses the thoracic inlet twice.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Duct</th><th>Drains</th><th>Empties into</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Right lymphatic duct (minor)</td><td>Right arm, right head and neck, right chest, <b>heart</b>, all lung lobes except the left upper</td><td>Right brachiocephalic vein, or right internal jugular–subclavian junction (variable)</td></tr><tr><td>Thoracic duct (left, major)</td><td><b>Both legs</b>, abdomen, pelvis, left arm, left head, neck and chest</td><td>Left internal jugular–subclavian junction (consistent)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Exam pattern: a \"left duct\" question hides the answer on the right side below the diaphragm (<b>right leg</b>, right kidney). A \"right duct\" question offers the right arm, right face, heart or right lung.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O082-18.jpg","cap":"Right lymphatic duct territory: right arm, right head, heart and lungs","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 18"},{"src":"sl/O082-14.jpg","cap":"Lymphangion valves keep lymph moving one way","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 14"}]},{"h":"What moves lymph","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Extrinsic forces</th><th>Intrinsic forces</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment), exercise, skeletal muscle contraction, adjacent artery pulsation, breathing (negative intrathoracic pressure)</td><td>Lymphatic smooth muscle contraction; interstitial fluid pressure</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Interstitial pressure is normally about <b>−6.3 mmHg</b> (flow about 120 cc/hr). Rising pressure pushes more fluid into lymph capillaries, but above <b>0 mmHg the capillaries collapse</b> and flow falls. Fluid entry rises with higher capillary pressure, lower plasma oncotic pressure, more interstitial protein and leakier capillaries. Hypertension, cirrhosis (low protein synthesis), starvation (low albumin) and toxins push pressure above 0.</p><p>Sympathetic nervous system (SNS): the lymph capillary is under SNS control. Acute SNS firing (alpha receptors) speeds lymphangion contraction; the SNS changes the rate but does not start peristalsis; beta receptors relax lymphatic muscle. <b>Chronic hypersympathetic tone</b> keeps vessels constricted and <b>reduces lymph movement</b>, as in post-mastectomy lymphedema. So treatment aims to <b>lower</b> sympathetic tone. Lymph nodes release more lymphocytes as SNS tone rises.</p><p><b>Physical activity</b> gives the largest rise in lymph return. In dogs, a lymphatic pump raised thoracic duct flow from 1.57 to 4.8 mL/min; exercise raised it from 1.47 to 5.81 mL/min.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O064-48.jpg","cap":"Chronic hypersympathetic tone reduces lymph flow: post-mastectomy lymphedema","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 48"}]},{"h":"The five diaphragms","html":"<p>A diaphragm is a thin partition of muscle and connective tissue between two regions. <b>Transverse diaphragms</b> create the pressure differences that move lymph and steady the vertical myofascial structures. The 2026 lecture teaches <b>five</b>. The review handout lists four (no popliteal); use the lecture's five.</p><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Diaphragm</th><th>Made of</th><th>Lymph point</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><b>Tentorium cerebelli</b> (+ diaphragma sellae)</td><td>Dura; the diaphragma sellae roofs the pituitary in the sella turcica</td><td>Uppermost; treated with cranial osteopathy</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Thoracic inlet (cervicothoracic)</td><td>Rib 1, T1, manubrium, clavicles + Sibson's fascia</td><td>Terminal drain for both ducts</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Abdominal (thoraco-abdominal, respiratory)</td><td>Muscle + central tendon; the <b>largest</b></td><td>Main pump; \"acts as a piston\"</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Pelvic</td><td>Levator ani + coccygeus with their fascia</td><td>Moves in step with the abdominal diaphragm; pumps pelvic, rectal and perineal lymph</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><b>Popliteal</b></td><td>Fascia of the popliteal fossa</td><td>Lymph capillaries crowded by the <b>soleus arch</b>; released with popliteal spread (direct myofascial release)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Not diaphragms (common distractors): <b>mediastinum</b> (a vertical space), broad ligament, greater omentum, hard and soft palate, tongue.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O082-47.jpg","cap":"Popliteal diaphragm: soleus arch can crowd the lymph capillaries","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 47"}]},{"h":"Thoraco-abdominal diaphragm","html":"<p>A dome with two hemidiaphragms, shaped by the organs below. Main job: breathing. Second jobs: blood and <b>lymph pumping</b>, speech, urination, defecation, childbirth.</p><p>Attachments: xiphoid, inner surfaces of ribs 6–12, T12, and the crura: <b>right crus L1–L3</b> (longer, stronger) and <b>left crus L1–L2</b>. The fibers meet in the <b>central tendon, which has no bony attachment</b>.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Opening</th><th>What inspiration does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>T8</td><td>Inferior vena cava</td><td>Vena cava widens; more venous blood enters the chest</td></tr><tr><td>T10</td><td><b>Esophagus</b></td><td>Contraction pinches the hiatus, preventing reflux</td></tr><tr><td>T12</td><td>Aorta + <b>thoracic duct</b>, behind the diaphragm</td><td>Both protected; a hypertonic hemidiaphragm can impair duct flow</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Memory aid: <b>\"I 8 10 Eggs At 12\"</b> = Inferior vena cava T8, Esophagus T10, Aorta T12.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Arcuate ligament</th><th>Arches over</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Median</td><td>Aorta (joins the two crura at T12)</td></tr><tr><td>Medial</td><td><b>Psoas</b></td></tr><tr><td>Lateral</td><td><b>Quadratus lumborum</b> (QL)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Psoas and QL fascia and the arcuate ligaments all attach to the <b>tip of rib 12</b>, so psoas, QL and lower-rib problems pull on the diaphragm.</p><p>Mechanics: on inspiration the diaphragm <b>contracts and descends</b>. <b>Intrathoracic pressure turns negative</b> while abdominal pressure rises; this gradient draws lymph and venous blood toward the heart. With the central tendon fixed, the lower ribs rise, then the upper ribs and sternum. The chest grows vertically (tendon descent), side to side (lower 6 ribs) and front to back (sternum).</p><p>Dysfunction: a <b>hypertonic diaphragm flattens</b>. The chest narrows, breathing is less efficient, the pressure gradient drops, lymph and venous return fall, and accessory muscles (scalenes) take over. Lateral curves (S or C) pull it unevenly. COPD (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease), asthma, obesity and kyphosis (ankylosing spondylitis, osteoporosis) all weaken the gradient.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O082-30.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm apertures T8, T10, T12 and the arcuate ligaments","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 30"}]},{"h":"Thoracic inlet, Sibson's fascia and cervical fascia","html":"<p>The thoracic inlet is bordered by <b>rib 1, T1, the manubrium and the clavicles</b>. T1–T4 and rib 2 also affect it. The interactive session calls the same region the \"thoracic outlet\". <b>Sibson's fascia</b> (suprapleural membrane) caps the lung apex. It is made of <b>prevertebral fascia and scalene fascia</b>; the prevertebral layer covers the longus colli. It acts as a cervicothoracic diaphragm.</p><p><b>Both ducts pass through it</b>, so an inlet restriction can cause <b>edema of the whole body</b>. A right-sided restriction mainly backs up the right duct's territory (right arm). Tight scalenes can impair lymph return. With inhalation, normal motion of rib 1 and the sternum helps the ducts empty, so inlet somatic dysfunction is an easy, high-yield target.</p><p>The anterior cervical fascia wraps the larynx, pharynx and thyroid and is intimate with head, neck and arm lymphatics. Cervical fascia moves <b>forward on exhalation and back on inhalation</b>. Fascia is continuous throughout the body and carries vessels, lymphatics and nerves, so any fascial strain slows them.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O082-41.jpg","cap":"Thoracic inlet borders, Sibson's fascia, thoracic duct to C7","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 41"}]},{"h":"Assessment and treatment order","html":"<p>Assess the thoracic inlet, abdominal and pelvic diaphragms, cervical, thoracic and lumbar spine, rib cage, anterior abdominal wall (transversus abdominis), posterior wall (iliopsoas), sacrum, pelvis and cranium. Congestion feels like <b>fullness and bogginess</b>: supraclavicular (head and neck), posterior axillary fold (arm), epigastric (abdominal organs), inguinal, popliteal and Achilles (leg and foot).</p><p>Rule: <b>remove restrictions first, then augment flow</b>. Start at the <b>central</b> drainage sites and move to the <b>periphery</b>. Direct or indirect techniques both work.</p><ol><li>Diagnose and treat <b>thoracic inlet</b> somatic dysfunction.</li><li><b>Re-dome</b> (relax) the abdominal diaphragm.</li><li>Treat the <b>pelvic diaphragm</b>.</li><li>Apply a <b>lymphatic pump</b>.</li><li>Then decongest target areas (Galbreath, effleurage, posterior axillary fold).</li></ol><p>Lab order: seated rib raising, thoracic inlet release, re-doming, pectoral lift, thoracic pump, cervical effleurage, sinus drainage, pedal pump, self-techniques.</p>","figs":[]},{"h":"Lymphatic techniques","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Hands</th><th>How it works</th><th>Clue / use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Thoracic inlet myofascial release</td><td>Thumbs on T1 and its transverse process, fingers on the clavicles</td><td>Find ease in rotation, side-bending, front-back; hold (indirect) until release</td><td>First step; upper respiratory infection, tight scalenes, rib 1 dysfunction</td></tr><tr><td>Re-doming the diaphragm</td><td>Fingers <b>under the costal margin</b>, pointing up</td><td>Follow up on exhalation, resist descent on inhalation</td><td>Flattened, hypertonic diaphragm</td></tr><tr><td>Seated rib raising</td><td>Fingers medial to the rib angles</td><td>Lift anterior and lateral</td><td>Rib motion; calms sympathetic chain ganglia</td></tr><tr><td>Pectoral lift (traction)</td><td>Grasp pectorals at the <b>anterior axillary folds</b>, lean back</td><td>Holds chest in inhalation; deep breaths work the diaphragm</td><td>Not the posterior axillary fold technique</td></tr><tr><td>Thoracic pump (Miller)</td><td>Palms on the upper chest</td><td>Push in exhalation (about 100–120/min); release version: hold on inhalation, then <b>let go suddenly</b> → elastic recoil, abrupt negative pressure</td><td>1918 influenza protocol</td></tr><tr><td>Pedal pump (Dalrymple)</td><td>Palms on the soles</td><td>Rhythmic dorsiflexion; whole-body effect</td><td>Rate \"not too fast, not too slow\"</td></tr><tr><td>Splenic / liver pump</td><td>Left / right lower ribs</td><td>Rhythmic compression</td><td>Immune effect</td></tr><tr><td>Cervical and sinus effleurage</td><td>Light strokes: frontal, supraorbital, infraorbital, inframalar, mandibular, submandibular; 3–4 sweeps each</td><td>Stroke in the direction of lymph flow</td><td>Sinus congestion</td></tr><tr><td>Sinus inhibitory pressure</td><td>Steady pressure on supraorbital, infraorbital and mental foramina (trigeminal nerve exits)</td><td>Reflex decongestion</td><td>Sinusitis</td></tr><tr><td><b>Galbreath</b> (mandibular drainage)</td><td>Hand on the mandible</td><td>Rhythmic down-and-medial pull opens the <b>Eustachian tube</b></td><td><b>Otitis media</b></td></tr><tr><td>Popliteal spread</td><td>Fingers in the popliteal fossa</td><td>Direct myofascial release spreading the fascia</td><td>Leg edema, cellulitis of the leg</td></tr></tbody></table>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O066-12.jpg","cap":"Re-doming: fingers under costal margin, seated or supine","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/O066-14.jpg","cap":"Miller thoracic pump and its sudden-release rebound version","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 14"}]},{"h":"Contraindications","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Relative (2026 lecture)</th><th>Absolute (2026 lecture)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unstaged cancer; coagulopathy or anticoagulants; abscess</td><td>DVT (deep vein thrombosis); <b>splenic pump in infectious mononucleosis</b>; local fracture; anuria in patients not on dialysis</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The course's OMT indications table adds absolute ones: necrotizing fasciitis, embolism, and techniques directly on infected nodes in overwhelming bacterial infection. Patient refusal rules out any OMT. Local infection (cellulitis, abscess, osteomyelitis) rules out working directly on that site: a diabetic foot with a positive probe-to-bone test means <b>no pedal pump</b>. Lab cautions: thoracic pump (rib fracture, severe osteoporosis, lung cancer); pedal pump (acute ankle sprain, DVT).</p>","figs":[]},{"h":"Clinical picture","html":"<p><b>Post-mastectomy lymphedema</b> (lab case: arm swelling after breast surgery with node removal). Check both arm circumferences, look for axillary cording (abduct the shoulder, extend the elbow, feel a taut cord), and test <b>Stemmer sign</b> (a skin fold at the base of the second finger cannot be lifted = lymphedema). Plan: lower sympathetic tone, open the inlet, re-dome the diaphragm, treat the pelvic diaphragm, then pump. Every one of these belongs in the plan.</p><p><b>1918 influenza</b>: DOs (doctors of osteopathic medicine) used the thoracic pump. Reported mortality: influenza 0.25% vs 5–6% with standard care; pneumonia 10% vs 33%. </p>","figs":[]}],"mk":["<b>Right lymphatic duct</b>: right arm, right head and neck, heart, lungs except left upper lobe; everything else goes to the thoracic duct.","<b>Thoracic duct</b>: starts at the cisterna chyli (L1–L2), passes the aortic hiatus at T12, rises to C7, empties at the left jugular–subclavian junction.","Diaphragm apertures: <b>T8 vena cava, T10 esophagus, T12 aorta + thoracic duct</b>.","<b>Lateral arcuate ligament</b> over quadratus lumborum; medial over psoas; median over aorta.","<b>Five diaphragms</b> (2026): tentorium cerebelli, thoracic inlet, abdominal (largest), pelvic, popliteal.","Inhalation: diaphragm descends → <b>negative intrathoracic pressure</b> → lymph drawn toward the chest.","<b>Sibson's fascia</b> = prevertebral (longus colli) + scalene fascia; both ducts cross it, so restriction can swell the whole body.","Thoracic inlet borders: <b>rib 1, T1, manubrium, clavicles</b>.","Order: <b>thoracic inlet → abdominal diaphragm → pelvic diaphragm → pump</b>; central before peripheral.","<b>Chronic hypersympathetic tone lowers lymph flow</b> (post-mastectomy lymphedema); treatment reduces it.","<b>Physical activity</b> increases lymph return more than any pump technique.","Absolute: deep vein thrombosis (DVT), <b>splenic pump in mononucleosis</b>, local fracture, anuria without dialysis; relative: unstaged cancer, anticoagulation, abscess.","<b>Miller thoracic pump</b>: chest compressions on exhalation, sudden release → recoil; 1918 influenza.","<b>Galbreath</b> → otitis media; <b>pectoral traction</b> → anterior axillary folds; <b>re-doming</b> → fingers under costal margin.","<b>Popliteal diaphragm</b>: soleus arch crowds lymph; popliteal spread releases the popliteal fossa fascia."],"tr":["Medial arcuate (psoas) vs lateral arcuate (quadratus lumborum) vs median arcuate (aorta).","T8 vena cava vs T10 esophagus vs T12 aorta and thoracic duct.","Acute sympathetic firing (faster lymph pumping) vs chronic hypersympathetic tone (less flow).","Anterior axillary fold (pectoral traction) vs posterior axillary fold (upper-limb drainage technique).","The left (thoracic) duct drains the right leg; the right duct drains the heart.","Galbreath (mandible, Eustachian tube) vs sinus effleurage or inhibitory pressure (face, sinuses).","Splenic pump: absolute no in mononucleosis; pedal pump: no on an infected foot.","Inlet is treated first; pumps and the periphery come last."]},"OMM-W12":{"bp":"The lumbar spine is built for weight bearing: sagittal facets make flexion–extension its main motion, and most of that happens at L5–S1. The lordosis, the Ferguson angle and the disc respond to posture and muscle pull, and the lower lumbar levels inside the multifidus triangle are where most low back pain starts. Lumbar somatic dysfunction is named with Fryette's principles from the posterior transverse process and how it changes with flexion and extension.","sec":[{"h":"Lumbar anatomy and the facets","html":"<p>The lumbar spine has <b>5 vertebrae</b> (anatomic L1–L5), but functionally it runs <b>T11–L5</b>. The bodies grow larger as you descend; L5 is the largest and has the most congenital anomalies. The design balances <b>stability and mobility</b> under body weight. The paired <b>zygapophyseal (facet) joints</b> are synovial; the <b>intervertebral disc</b> joints are fibrocartilaginous.</p><p><b>Facets.</b> Superior articular facets are concave and face <b>back, up and medial</b> (mnemonic <b>BUM</b>); inferior facets are convex and face forward, down and lateral. Thoracic superior facets differ: back, up and lateral. Lumbar facets sit close to the <b>sagittal plane</b> (the lecture notes they turn about 45° from it). Sagittal joint surfaces glide forward and back but block twisting, so <b>flexion–extension is the main motion</b> and rotation the least.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Motion</th><th>Normal range</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flexion</td><td>40–50° (IA, interactive session: 40–60°)</td><td>Largest; facets glide in the sagittal plane</td></tr><tr><td>Extension</td><td>20–30°</td><td>Facets close; anterior longitudinal ligament limits it</td></tr><tr><td>Sidebending</td><td>About 20–30° per side</td><td>In between</td></tr><tr><td>Rotation</td><td>About 5–7° per side</td><td>Least; sagittal facets lock it</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Facet tropism</b> means the left and right facets at one level have different angles. It is the most common lumbar anomaly and predisposes to early degeneration. It is anatomy you are born with, not a result of posture.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O068-7.jpg","cap":"Superior facets concave, facing back and medial; inferior facets convex, facing lateral","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 7"}]},{"h":"Curves, Ferguson's angle and the muscles that set them","html":"<p>Lumbar <b>lordosis</b> develops in childhood: the spine extends while the iliopsoas fails to lengthen fully. Lordosis depends on the <b>sacral base</b>, and the capsule of the hip also influences the lumbosacral angle.</p><p><b>Ferguson's (lumbosacral) angle</b> is the angle between a <b>horizontal line</b> and a line along the <b>top of the sacrum</b> (the S1 superior endplate), measured on a standing lateral X-ray. The lecture gives <b>25–35°</b> as normal; the IA slide quotes 30–45° and calls <b>&gt;45° hyperlordotic</b>. A larger angle means a steeper sacral base, <b>hyperlordosis</b> and more <b>shear at the L5–S1 junction</b>, where most degeneration and pain arise. Pelvic rotation changes the angle: anterior pelvic tilt raises it, posterior tilt lowers it.</p><p>In the lecture's curve figure, A is normal. <b>B is kypholordotic</b>: deeper curves, a larger angle, spinous processes and facets compressed, anterior longitudinal ligament stretched. <b>C is flattened</b>, with a smaller angle.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Muscle</th><th>Action on pelvis or spine</th><th>Lordosis and angle</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Psoas</td><td>Pulls the upper lumbar vertebrae forward (think arching the back)</td><td>Increases</td></tr><tr><td>Quadriceps (hypertonic)</td><td>Tilts the pelvis anteriorly</td><td>Increases</td></tr><tr><td>Gluteus maximus and hamstrings</td><td>Pull the pelvis posteriorly</td><td>Decreases (hypolordotic)</td></tr><tr><td>Abdominals (obliques, transversus via the lumbodorsal fascia; rectus)</td><td>Stabilize the spine</td><td>Decreases (flattens)</td></tr></tbody></table>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O068-10.jpg","cap":"Ferguson's angle between sacral base and horizontal; normal 25–35°","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 10"}]},{"h":"Muscles, ligaments and the multifidus triangle","html":"<p>Muscle length predicts the type of <b>somatic dysfunction (SD)</b> a muscle holds. <b>Long muscles</b> pull a group of segments into a curve (<b>type I</b>). <b>Short, deep muscles</b> get innervation from the fewest spinal segments and control single vertebrae, so they lock one segment (<b>type II</b>).</p><table><thead><tr><th>Muscle</th><th>Span</th><th>Action</th><th>SD it maintains</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Erector spinae: spinalis, longissimus, iliocostalis (medial to lateral)</td><td>Long, many segments</td><td>Both sides: extension. One side: same-side extension and sidebending</td><td>Type I group curve</td></tr><tr><td>Quadratus lumborum</td><td>Iliac crest and iliolumbar ligament to rib 12 and L1–L4 transverse processes</td><td>Sidebending; hip hiking</td><td>Type I group curve</td></tr><tr><td>Multifidus, rotatores (brevis spans one level), intertransversarii</td><td>Short, deep, 1–2 segments</td><td>Local extension and rotation; posture</td><td>Type II single segment</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Latissimus dorsi links the lumbar fascia to the arm; its problems give arm and shoulder dysesthesias.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Ligament</th><th>Feature</th><th>Clinical point</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Anterior longitudinal</td><td>Broad; limits extension</td><td>Stretched in hyperlordosis</td></tr><tr><td>Posterior longitudinal (PLL)</td><td>Narrow; limits flexion; at L4–L5 half its L1 width</td><td>Leaves the disc weak posterolaterally</td></tr><tr><td>Interspinous and supraspinous</td><td>Between spinous processes, behind the disc</td><td>Segmental tenderness here suggests a <b>possible disc problem</b></td></tr><tr><td>Iliolumbar</td><td>L4–L5 transverse processes to iliac crest just above and lateral to the PSIS (posterior superior iliac spine); blends with the anterior sacroiliac ligament</td><td><b>First ligament strained</b> in lumbosacral imbalance; found by <b>spring test</b>; refers pain to the <b>groin</b>; mimics <b>sacroiliac (SI) joint</b> dysfunction</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The <b>multifidus triangle</b> runs from the L4 spinous process to both iliac crests and down to the sacral tip. Most low back problems live here: multifidi, the most commonly ruptured <b>L4–L5 discs</b>, intertransverse and iliolumbar ligaments, facet syndromes and the SI joint. Palpating it gives your baseline.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O068-36.jpg","cap":"Multifidus triangle: L4 spine, iliac crests and sacral tip enclose common pain sources","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 36"}]},{"h":"Lumbar exam: flexion and screening tests","html":"<p>The exam runs: observation, palpation, active and passive motion, auscultation and percussion, segmental exam, special tests. Include an <b>abdominal exam</b>, because viscera refer pain to the lumbar spine (for example kidney and ureter at T10–L2).</p><p><b>Flexion</b> totals about 45–50°. <b>L5–S1 gives 60–75%</b>, L4–L5 20–25% (together 80–90%), and L1–L4 only 5–10%. Full flexion also needs the <b>pelvis to rotate about the hip joints</b>, so check the hips and hamstrings when flexion is lost. In lab, the lordosis should flatten on forward bending. A paraspinal prominence that disappears when seated comes from the lower extremities.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>How</th><th>Normal</th><th>Positive means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Standing sidebending</td><td>Slide a hand down the lateral thigh</td><td>Smooth curve, 20–25°</td><td>Sharp angle or flat segment: SD</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hip drop</b></td><td>Standing, bend one knee with the heel down; fingers on the iliac crests</td><td>Crest drops 20–25°; lumbar spine sidebends smoothly <b>away</b> from the dropped side</td><td>Drop under 20° (IA: or over 25°), or a non-smooth curve. <b>Positive side = the side that drops less</b></td></tr><tr><td>Trunk rotation</td><td>Screens thoracolumbar rotation</td><td>Symmetric</td><td>Limited range</td></tr><tr><td>Segmental tenderness</td><td>Press spinous process and interspinous ligament</td><td>Not tender</td><td>Possible disc issue</td></tr><tr><td>Iliolumbar spring</td><td>Spring between the L4–L5 transverse processes and iliac crest</td><td>Not tender</td><td>Iliolumbar ligament irritation</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Hip drop logic: bending the right knee drops the right crest and the lumbar spine sidebends <b>left</b>. So a positive right hip drop means <b>restricted left sidebending</b>, as in L1–5 NSrRL (neutral, sidebent right, rotated left). The lecture also lists the test for gluteus medius weakness.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O069-58.jpg","cap":"Hip drop: crest drops 20–25°, lumbar spine sidebends smoothly away from the drop","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 58"}]},{"h":"Fryette's principles and naming lumbar dysfunction","html":"<p>Rotation is named for the <b>front of the vertebral body</b>. Body rotated right means the <b>right transverse process (TP) is posterior</b> and the spinous process (SP) swings <b>left</b>. SD is named for its <b>freedoms</b> (the position it rests in); the <b>barriers are the opposite motions</b>.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Type I (neutral)</th><th>Type II (non-neutral)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fryette's principle</td><td>First: from neutral, sidebending comes before rotation</td><td>Second: in flexion or extension, rotation comes before sidebending</td></tr><tr><td>Coupling</td><td>Opposite sides</td><td><b>Same side</b>; rotates into the concavity</td></tr><tr><td>Extent</td><td>Group of segments</td><td>Single segment; neutral mechanics above and below</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>L1–4 NSlRr</td><td>L4 FRrSr (flexed, rotated and sidebent right)</td></tr><tr><td>Flexion or extension</td><td>Asymmetry unchanged</td><td>Asymmetry <b>worse toward the barrier</b></td></tr><tr><td>Typical muscle</td><td>Long (erector spinae, quadratus lumborum)</td><td>Short (rotatores, multifidus)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Fryette's third principle: motion in one plane reduces motion in the others. A flexed segment has less sidebending and rotation.</p><p><b>Flexion–extension test.</b> Find the posterior TP, then flex and extend. A <b>flexed</b> segment (FRS) cannot extend, so its asymmetry is <b>worse in extension</b> and evens out in flexion. An <b>extended</b> segment (ERS) is <b>worse in flexion</b>. Example: right TP of L2 more posterior in flexion means L2 ERRSR.</p><p><b>Springing (prone, anterior pressure).</b> Pressure on the SP pushes the segment into extension; resistance means it is <b>flexed</b>. Pressure on the right TP rotates it left; resistance means it is <b>rotated right</b>. Both resisted means FRSR.</p><p><b>Injury pattern.</b> Bending forward and to the right leaves a segment stuck in FRSR. It is restricted in <b>extension, left rotation and left sidebending</b>.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O068-60.jpg","cap":"Right rotation: right transverse process goes posterior, spinous process swings left","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/O068-64.jpg","cap":"Non-neutral RrSr segment rotates into the concavity; neutral mechanics above and below","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 64"}]},{"h":"The intervertebral disc","html":"<p>Lumbar discs are large and built to dissipate heavy loads. Each is named for the vertebra above. The <b>annulus fibrosus</b> has crosshatched layers that allow rotation and shear while staying stable; it is <b>thicker anteriorly and thinner posteriorly</b>. The <b>nucleus pulposus</b> sits in the center.</p><p><b>Squish and squirt:</b> the nucleus moves <b>away from the compressed side</b>, like toothpaste. Flexion squeezes the front, so the nucleus moves <b>posteriorly</b> and loads the posterior annulus and PLL; this worsens a bulging or herniated disc. Extension moves it <b>anteriorly</b> and loads the anterior annulus. Sidebending right moves it left, <b>toward the convexity</b>.</p><p>A disc thins under load during the day and reabsorbs fluid when unloaded at night; age slows recovery. A diseased or aged disc is more compressible, its annulus tears, and lost disc height shifts load onto the facets.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Position (70 kg person)</th><th>Load on L3 disc</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Supine</td><td>About 25 kg (lowest)</td></tr><tr><td>Lateral recumbent</td><td>About 75 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Standing</td><td>About 100 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Sitting</td><td>About 140 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Standing flexed</td><td>About 150 kg</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sitting flexed</b></td><td>About 185 kg (<b>highest</b>)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In newtons: standing 700, twisting 900, sidebending 950, upright sitting 1000. Lifting 20 kg with the back bent and knees straight gives 3400, versus 2100 with the back straight and knees bent.</p><p><b>Herniation</b> goes <b>posterolateral</b> because the PLL is narrow there. <b>L4–L5 and L5–S1</b> account for about 95% of lumbar herniations. In a protrusion the nucleus bulges the annulus; in a prolapse it breaks through to press on a nerve root.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O069-111.jpg","cap":"Disc load by position: supine lowest, seated flexed highest","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 111"}]},{"h":"Too much or too little lordosis","html":"<p>Both extremes cause low back pain, by opposite mechanisms. Extension closes the intervertebral foramina and approximates the facets; flexion opens them.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Hyperlordosis (lordotic low back pain)</th><th>Loss of lordosis (flat back)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Typical cause</td><td>Hypertonic psoas or quadriceps, weak abdominals, larger Ferguson's angle</td><td>Long periods in forward flexion</td></tr><tr><td>Facet joints</td><td>Compressed: <b>degenerative changes</b></td><td>Gapped</td></tr><tr><td>Intervertebral foramen</td><td><b>Narrowed</b></td><td>Opened</td></tr><tr><td>Nerve roots and sheaths</td><td><b>More prone to irritation</b></td><td>Pressed by a posterior disc bulge</td></tr><tr><td>Disc nucleus</td><td>Pushed anteriorly</td><td>Pushed posteriorly: bulging and posterior herniation</td></tr><tr><td>Soft tissue</td><td>Anterior longitudinal ligament stretched</td><td>Posterior ligaments, fascia and muscles elongated</td></tr><tr><td>Exercise</td><td>Supine pelvic tilt; active resistive flexion (roll into a ball, push knees up)</td><td>Extension-inducing exercises (prone)</td></tr></tbody></table>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O069-40.jpg","cap":"Extension closes the foramina and approximates the facets; flexion opens them","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 40"}]},{"h":"Treatment and the low back pain patient","html":"<p><b>Lumbar HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude)</b>, from DiGiovanna's text Figure 54-1: the patient lies on the side with the <b>posterior transverse process up</b>, the physician in front with one forearm on the shoulder and one on the pelvis. It treats a type II segment, after myofascial release of the area. HVLA is relatively contraindicated with an acute disc herniation and with spondylolisthesis.</p><p><b>Red flags</b> (IA): age under 20 or over 55, night pain, pain unrelated to activity, fever, unexplained weight loss, history of cancer, HIV, steroids or intravenous drug use, significant trauma, widespread neurologic signs, and failure to improve after 4–6 weeks. <b>Cauda equina syndrome</b> (saddle anesthesia, bladder or bowel dysfunction, bilateral sciatica) is a surgical emergency.</p><p><b>NEJM 1999 trial</b> (Andersson): 155 patients with subacute low back pain (3 weeks to 6 months) received osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) or standard care for 12 weeks. Both groups improved equally on the main outcomes, but the OMT group used <b>significantly less medication</b> (analgesics, anti-inflammatories, muscle relaxants) and less physical therapy. Over 90% in each group were satisfied.</p>","figs":[]}],"mk":["<b>Sagittal facets</b> (superior facets back, up, medial: BUM): flexion–extension is the main lumbar motion; rotation is the least.","<b>Ferguson's angle</b> = sacral base line vs horizontal; normal 25–35° (IA: &gt;45° hyperlordotic); larger = hyperlordosis + L5–S1 shear.","<b>L5–S1</b> gives 60–75% of lumbar flexion; full flexion also needs the pelvis to rotate about the hips.","Gluteus maximus, hamstrings and abdominals <b>decrease</b> lordosis; psoas and quadriceps <b>increase</b> it.","<b>Long muscles</b> (erector spinae, quadratus lumborum) hold type I group curves; <b>short muscles</b> (rotatores, multifidus) hold type II single segments.","<b>Iliolumbar ligament</b>: L4–L5 to iliac crest; first strained; spring test; groin referral; mimics sacroiliac dysfunction.","<b>Hip drop</b>: crest drops 20–25°, spine sidebends away; positive = the side that drops less (L1–5 NSrRL: positive right).","Type II: rotation and sidebending to the <b>same side</b>, single segment; type I: opposite sides, group.","SD is named for its freedoms; <b>barriers are the opposites</b> (bent forward and right: FRSR, restricted extension, left rotation, left sidebending).","<b>Posterior TP = side of rotation</b>; the spinous process swings the opposite way.","Asymmetry gets <b>worse toward the barrier</b>: FRS worse in extension, ERS worse in flexion.","Springing: resisted anterior push on the SP = <b>flexed</b>; resisted push on the right TP = <b>rotated right</b>.","The nucleus moves <b>away from compression</b>: posterior in flexion, toward the convexity in sidebending.","Disc load is highest <b>sitting flexed</b>, lowest supine; herniations are <b>posterolateral at L4–L5 and L5–S1</b>.","Hyperlordosis: facet <b>degeneration</b>, <b>narrowed</b> foramina, nerve roots <b>more prone to irritation</b>; lumbar HVLA sets the posterior TP <b>up</b>."],"tr":["Hip drop: the positive side is the side that drops less, but the restricted motion is sidebending to the <b>opposite</b> side.","FRS is worse in <b>extension</b>, ERS is worse in <b>flexion</b>: the asymmetry grows toward the barrier, not toward the name.","Posterior TP is on the side of rotation; the spinous process deviates to the <b>opposite</b> side.","Erector spinae (long) cause <b>type I</b>; rotatores and multifidus (short) cause <b>type II</b>.","Extension closes the foramina and loads the facets; flexion opens the foramina but pushes the nucleus <b>posteriorly</b>.","Iliolumbar ligament pain (groin, spring test) vs the sacroiliac joint dysfunction it mimics.","Ferguson's angle: lecture normal 25–35° vs IA normal 30–45° with &gt;45° hyperlordotic.","Sitting (about 140 kg) loads the disc more than standing (100 kg); standing flexed (150 kg) is still less than sitting flexed (185 kg)."]},"OMM-W13":{"bp":"Four muscles tie the lumbar spine to the ribs, pelvis and hip: the diaphragm, quadratus lumborum, psoas and piriformis. A tight one bends the lumbar spine in a predictable way and leaves a predictable set of findings — the psoas syndrome pattern is the most tested. Lecture 13B adds Travell’s trigger points, which refer pain to a distant zone, and how they differ from Jones tender points and Chapman’s reflex points.","sec":[{"h":"Diaphragm: attachments, nerves and the lumbar link","html":"<p>The diaphragm attaches to the <b>lower ribs</b> and the <b>sternum</b> (xiphoid). Its two <b>crura</b> anchor on the bodies of <b>L1, L2 and L3</b> (right crus to L3, left to L2). The psoas and quadratus lumborum (QL) pass behind it under its arches.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Nerve</th><th>Roots</th><th>What it carries</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Phrenic</td><td><b>C3–C5</b></td><td>All motor; sensation from the central part</td></tr><tr><td>Intercostals</td><td>T5–T11</td><td>Sensation from the peripheral rim only</td></tr><tr><td>Subcostal</td><td>T12</td><td>Sensation from the peripheral rim only</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Mnemonic: “<b>C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive</b>.” Lookalikes: C5–C7 = long thoracic nerve; the vagus passes through but does not move it.</p><p>Link to the low back: upper lumbar somatic dysfunction (SD) can flatten the diaphragm; a tight psoas or QL shortens it; diaphragm spasm restricts lumbar motion.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O074-3.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm from below: crura descend onto the upper lumbar bodies","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 3"}]},{"h":"Quadratus lumborum (QL)","html":"<p>The QL joins the <b>12th rib</b> and the <b>L1–L4 transverse processes</b> to the <b>iliolumbar ligament</b> and <b>posterior iliac crest</b>. (Decks swap which end is the origin; learn the two ends.) Palpate it in the gap <b>between rib 12 and the iliac crest</b>, lateral to the erector spinae. Its trigger points sit at the <b>lateral edge</b>.</p><p>Actions: one side = <b>side-bending</b> and <b>hip hiking</b>; both sides = extension; it holds rib 12 down.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Hypertonic right QL: finding</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lumbar side-bent <b>right</b> (type I group curve, concave right)</td><td>The tight muscle shortens the right side</td></tr><tr><td><b>Decreased side-bending left</b> (and/or flexion)</td><td>Bending away stretches the tight muscle</td></tr><tr><td>Right leg <b>looks short</b> (functional short leg)</td><td>The QL hikes the right innominate</td></tr><tr><td>Gentle leg traction in supine restricted on the right</td><td>Traction tries to lengthen the QL</td></tr><tr><td>Hip drop test: right crest drops less than 20°</td><td>QL holds the crest up</td></tr><tr><td>Pain to sacroiliac (SI) joint, buttock, trochanter</td><td>Trigger-point referral</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Causes: overload or <b>prolonged slumped sitting</b>. Travell called QL trigger points the <b>most overlooked</b> muscle cause of low back pain; suspect them when rib 12 and SI dysfunction keep returning after osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT).</p><p><b>QL vs true short leg:</b> a right QL spasm can make the <b>right</b> leg look short and hide a true left short leg. Treat the QL first; the true short leg then shows and may need a heel lift.</p><table><thead><tr><th>QL treatment</th><th>How</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Passive myofascial, prone</td><td>Stand on the opposite side; fix rib 12; lift the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) toward you, slowly and rhythmically</td></tr><tr><td>Muscle energy</td><td>Side-lying, tight side up; longitudinal stretch separates rib 12 from the crest (barrier); patient <b>hikes the hip toward the head</b> against your counterforce</td></tr></tbody></table>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O074-11.jpg","cap":"Right QL spasm: right leg looks short, lumbar side-bent right","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 11"}]},{"h":"Iliopsoas: anatomy and the lordosis pull","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Muscle</th><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Nerve</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Psoas major</td><td>T12–L5 bodies, discs, transverse processes</td><td>Lesser trochanter (shared tendon)</td><td>L1–L2 (some L3)</td><td>Flexes thigh; some external rotation; flexes spine on pelvis</td></tr><tr><td>Psoas minor (absent in 40%)</td><td>T12–L1 bodies</td><td>Pecten pubis</td><td>L1</td><td>Weak flexor</td></tr><tr><td>Iliacus</td><td>Iliac fossa, inner iliac crest, ASIS, lateral sacrum</td><td>Lesser trochanter (shared tendon)</td><td>Femoral nerve branches (L1–L2)</td><td>Flexes thigh; moves trunk</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The iliopsoas is the <b>strongest hip flexor</b> (Travell’s “hidden prankster”). Its trigger points refer to the low back and <b>anterior thigh</b>.</p><p>The <b>ureter</b>, kidney, colon, sympathetic trunk and lumbar plexus lie on the psoas, so organ disease can cause psoas spasm.</p><p><b>Lordosis pull:</b> standing fixes the femur, so a short psoas pulls the lumbar spine into <b>extension (increased lordosis)</b>, draws the <b>sacral base forward</b> and rotates the pelvis <b>anteriorly</b>.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Muscle group</th><th>Effect on lumbar lordosis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Psoas (tight)</td><td><b>Increases</b> — pulls directly on the vertebrae</td></tr><tr><td>Quadriceps (tight)</td><td>Increases, but only indirectly, by tipping the pelvis</td></tr><tr><td>Gluteus maximus / hamstrings</td><td>Decrease — pull the pelvis posteriorly</td></tr><tr><td>Abdominals</td><td>Flatten it (weak abdominals let it increase)</td></tr></tbody></table>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O074-31.jpg","cap":"Short psoas with femur fixed: lumbar extension, anterior pelvic rotation","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 31"}]},{"h":"Psoas syndrome: cause and findings","html":"<p>Psoas syndrome is low back pain from <b>psoas hypertonicity</b> with a typical cluster of signs. The psoas is kept <b>short for a long time</b> (desk work, weeding, sit-ups), then <b>suddenly lengthened</b> (standing up fast). The spindles signal “overstretch,” alpha and gamma output rise, and a <b>pain–spasm cycle</b> follows.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Posture and gait</td><td>Flexed at the hip, <b>side-bent toward</b> the tighter psoas</td></tr><tr><td>Pain</td><td>Low back and front/side of thigh, <b>no lower than the knee</b></td></tr><tr><td>Range of motion</td><td>More hip flexion, <b>less hip extension</b> on the psoas side</td></tr><tr><td>Key lesion</td><td><b>Non-neutral flexion SD of L1 or L2</b> (sometimes T12 or L3), rotated and side-bent <b>toward</b> the psoas. Left psoas → L1 FRSL (flexed, rotated and side-bent left)</td></tr><tr><td>Other SD</td><td><b>L5 extended</b>; sacrum rotated on an oblique axis (often forward)</td></tr><tr><td>Tender points</td><td><b>Ipsilateral iliacus</b> (just medial to the ASIS); <b>contralateral piriformis</b></td></tr><tr><td>Pelvic shift</td><td>To the <b>opposite</b> side; named for the free side, so a left psoas gives a positive shift right</td></tr><tr><td>Tests</td><td><b>Thomas test</b> positive</td></tr><tr><td>Neuro exam</td><td><b>Normal</b> (unlike disc radiculopathy)</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Full-blown LEFT psoas syndrome: left side</th><th>Right (opposite) side</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Marked left psoas spasm; Thomas test positive left</td><td>Pelvis shifts right</td></tr><tr><td>Key non-neutral SD at L1 or L2, side-bent left</td><td>Right piriformis spasm with tender point</td></tr><tr><td>Sacrum rotated on the left oblique axis (often forward)</td><td>Pain in the right hip, down the back of the thigh to the knee</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Memory line: <b>psoas side</b> = posture, Thomas test, L1–L2 rotation and side-bending; <b>opposite side</b> = piriformis, pelvic shift, leg pain.</p><p><b>Thomas test:</b> supine, hug one knee to the chest; if the other thigh lifts off the table, that iliopsoas is tight. Lookalikes: Thompson (Achilles rupture), Spurling (cervical root), Phalen (carpal tunnel).</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O078-17.jpg","cap":"Full-blown left psoas syndrome: the six-part pattern","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/O074-41.jpg","cap":"Positive Thomas test: the extended thigh lifts off the table","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 41"}]},{"h":"Organic causes: think of the viscera","html":"<p>Rule out organ disease before calling psoas spasm mechanical. Examples: ureter or kidney disease, appendicitis, sigmoid colon disease, inflammatory bowel disease, prostatitis, ovarian disease, cancer, psoas abscess, hip arthritis.</p><p><b>Kidney stone:</b> the ureter runs over the psoas, so a passing stone often causes psoas spasm. Look for an <b>acute viscerosomatic reflex</b>: <b>warm, boggy</b> paraspinal tissue at the organ’s sympathetic level, on that side. Chronic organ disease gives cool, ropy tissue instead.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Organ</th><th>Level of paraspinal changes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kidney, upper ureter</td><td>T10–T11</td></tr><tr><td>Appendix, cecum, ascending colon</td><td>T10–T11, right</td></tr><tr><td>Descending and sigmoid colon, lower ureter, bladder, prostate, uterus</td><td>T12–L2</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Appendicitis (Week 8 case): right psoas spasm, right T10–T11 changes, Chapman’s nodule at the <b>right 12th rib tip</b>.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O052-61.jpg","cap":"Appendicitis case: right psoas spasm, Chapman's nodule at right 12th rib tip","where":"2026 week 8 Lecture 8 Autonomics (New ANS Lecture), slide 61"}]},{"h":"Treating psoas syndrome","html":"<p>Goal: restore the normal lumbar front-to-back curve. <b>Treat the key lesion first</b> — the upper lumbar (L1–L2) flexion SD. This alone may restore function. Treat any organ cause too.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Use</th><th>Avoid</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Acute</td><td>Only techniques that <b>extend</b> the upper lumbar: passive extension (rolled towel at the thoracolumbar junction), counterstrain, muscle energy, gentle myofascial; ice; NSAIDs (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs), muscle relaxants</td><td><b>HVLA</b> (high-velocity low-amplitude thrust), which is not tolerated; heat at first; over-treating; the unstable <b>lumbosacral</b> area</td></tr><tr><td>Chronic</td><td>More vigorous articulation for fibrosis; muscle energy; myofascial</td><td>Repeated HVLA at the lumbosacral junction</td></tr><tr><td>Home</td><td>Psoas stretches (lunge, pelvis rolled forward), towel extension stretch, swimming, staying active</td><td>In a flare: sleeping prone, slumping, toe touching, sit-ups</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Psoas muscle energy:</b> patient <b>supine with the affected leg off the table</b>, so the hip goes into extension (the barrier). The patient then flexes the hip against your counterforce. Marked hip flexion is ease, not barrier.</p>","figs":[]},{"h":"Piriformis and piriformis syndrome","html":"<p>The piriformis runs from the <b>anterior sacrum (S2–S4)</b> through the greater sciatic foramen to the <b>top of the greater trochanter</b>. Nerve: S1–S2. It is one of six hip lateral rotators.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Hip position</th><th>Piriformis action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Leg straight</td><td><b>External rotation</b> (some extension)</td></tr><tr><td>Hip flexed 90°</td><td><b>Abduction</b> (may internally rotate)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A hypertonic piriformis holds the leg <b>turned out</b>: external rotation is the freedom and <b>internal rotation is restricted</b>. Muscle energy therefore positions into <b>internal rotation</b> (the barrier), and the patient pushes toward external rotation.</p><p><b>Piriformis syndrome</b> is irritation of the <b>sciatic nerve</b> (L4–S3, the body’s largest nerve) by the piriformis. Usually secondary to trauma, overuse or compression (“wallet neuritis”); under 15% is primary (split muscle or nerve).</p><p>Clues: pain <b>after sitting 15–20 minutes</b>, buttock pain to the knee; leg turned out; tender sciatic notch and piriformis; forward sacral torsion to the opposite side; straight leg raise <b>painful throughout</b>; <b>normal neuro exam</b>.</p><p>Treatment: conservative care, stretching; OMT with counterstrain, muscle energy, piriformis inhibition.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Pain</th><th>Key sign</th><th>Neuro</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Psoas syndrome</td><td>Low back, front/side of thigh, not past knee</td><td>Flexed, side-bent toward psoas; Thomas positive</td><td>Normal</td></tr><tr><td>QL hypertonicity</td><td>Low back, buttock, trochanter</td><td>Side-bent toward QL; leg looks short; traction restricted</td><td>Normal</td></tr><tr><td>Piriformis syndrome</td><td>Buttock, back of thigh; worse sitting</td><td>Leg turned out; internal rotation restricted</td><td>Normal</td></tr><tr><td>L5/S1 disc radiculopathy</td><td>Below the knee to the foot</td><td>Dermatome, strength or reflex loss</td><td>Abnormal</td></tr></tbody></table>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O069-72.jpg","cap":"The six hip lateral rotators, piriformis highlighted on top","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 72"}]},{"h":"Trigger points (Travell)","html":"<p>A trigger point (TrP) is a <b>hyperirritable spot in a taut band</b> of skeletal muscle. Pressing it causes <b>referred pain</b>, motor dysfunction and autonomic effects; snapping palpation or needling gives a <b>local twitch response</b>. Mechanism: a faulty motor end plate releases <b>excess acetylcholine</b>, and sarcomeres stay locked as “contraction knots.”</p><p>Stages: hyperirritability → local ischemia → palpable taut band → <b>satellite TrPs</b> in the referral zone. The muscle shows pain, referred pain, <b>weakness</b> and <b>reduced flexibility</b>.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Active TrP</th><th>Latent TrP</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Spontaneous pain</td><td>Yes — it is the complaint</td><td>No; painful only when pressed</td></tr><tr><td>Pain pattern</td><td>Patient <b>recognizes</b> it</td><td>May not feel familiar</td></tr><tr><td>How common</td><td>Less common</td><td>More common</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both can cause autonomic signs (sweating, tearing, gooseflesh) and dizziness or imbalance.</p><p>Treatment: <b>spray and stretch</b> (ethyl chloride vapocoolant over the TrP and its referral zone — the spray distracts, the <b>stretch</b> treats; rewarm after), injection or dry needling, OMT (counterstrain, muscle energy). Always add <b>stretching and self-care</b>.</p><p><b>Pain at the greater trochanter:</b> local tenderness with inflamed tissue = bursitis. Little local tenderness = <b>referred pain</b> from QL, piriformis or <b>gluteus medius TrP 2</b>. Use the referral maps to pick muscles, then palpate for a taut band and twitch that reproduce the pain.</p><p><b>Right pectoralis major TrP</b> between ribs 5 and 6 near the sternum → <b>supraventricular tachycardia (SVT)</b>, a somatovisceral reflex; treating the TrP can stop it. Lookalike: a flattened thoracic kyphosis goes with mitral valve prolapse, not SVT.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O079-124.jpg","cap":"Right pectoralis major trigger point between ribs 5–6 linked to SVT","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 124"}]},{"h":"Trigger points vs tender points vs Chapman’s points","html":"<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Chapman’s point</th><th>Trigger point (Travell)</th><th>Tender point (Jones)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Location</td><td>Deep fascia / periosteum</td><td>Muscle</td><td>Muscle, tendon, ligament, fascia</td></tr><tr><td>Feel</td><td>Smooth, firm nodule, 2–3 mm (“pea”) up to almond size</td><td>Taut band; twitch response</td><td>Small, tense spot; no taut band</td></tr><tr><td>Pain</td><td>Slight to severe, local</td><td><b>Refers to a predictable zone</b></td><td>Local only; exquisitely tender</td></tr><tr><td>Reflex</td><td>Viscerosomatic</td><td>Viscerosomatic, somatovisceral or somatosomatic</td><td>Somatic dysfunction</td></tr><tr><td>Treatment</td><td>Firm rotary pressure, 20–30 seconds</td><td>Spray and stretch, injection, OMT, stretching</td><td>Counterstrain</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both trigger and tender points give a jump sign; only the TrP refers.</p><p><b>Chapman’s reflexes</b> are pea-sized nodules on the deep fascia that signal an organ problem through sympathetic-driven lymph congestion. They come in <b>anterior/posterior pairs</b>: anterior in the intercostal spaces beside the sternum, posterior between the spinous and transverse processes. The full chart comes next year; know these:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Organ</th><th>Anterior Chapman’s point</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Upper lung (and arm)</td><td>3rd intercostal space at the sternum</td></tr><tr><td>Lower lung</td><td>4th intercostal space at the sternum</td></tr><tr><td>Liver (right) / stomach acidity (left)</td><td>5th intercostal space</td></tr><tr><td>Liver–gallbladder (right) / stomach peristalsis (left)</td><td>6th intercostal space</td></tr><tr><td>Pancreas (right) / spleen (left)</td><td>7th intercostal space</td></tr><tr><td>Appendix</td><td><b>Tip of the right 12th rib</b></td></tr><tr><td>Colon</td><td><b>Lateral thigh</b> (along the iliotibial band)</td></tr></tbody></table>","figs":[]}],"mk":["<b>Phrenic nerve, C3–C5</b>, moves the diaphragm; its crura anchor on L1–L3.","Quadratus lumborum (QL) lies between <b>rib 12 and the posterior iliac crest</b>; it side-bends the spine and <b>hikes the hip</b>.","Tight QL: side-bent <b>toward</b> it; that leg <b>looks short</b>.","A short psoas (femur fixed) pulls the lumbar spine into <b>increased lordosis</b> and tips the pelvis forward.","Psoas syndrome: flexed and side-bent <b>toward</b> the psoas, <b>Thomas test positive</b>, pain not past the knee, normal neuro exam.","Key lesion: <b>L1 or L2 flexed</b>, rotated and side-bent toward the psoas (left psoas → L1 FRSL); L5 is extended.","Opposite side: <b>piriformis spasm</b> and <b>pelvic shift</b>; same side: iliacus tender point medial to the ASIS.","Treat the <b>L1–L2 key lesion first</b>; acute = extension only, no thrust.","Psoas muscle energy: <b>supine, affected leg off the table</b> (hip extension is the barrier).","Psoas spasm + warm, boggy right T10–T12 → <b>kidney stone</b> (ureter lies on the psoas).","Piriformis is an <b>external rotator</b>: freedom external rotation, barrier internal rotation.","Trigger point = <b>taut band, twitch, referred pain</b>; Jones tender point = local pain only.","<b>Right pectoralis major trigger point</b> (ribs 5–6, by the sternum) → supraventricular tachycardia.","Chapman’s point = small nodule on <b>deep fascia</b> (organ reflex); appendix = <b>tip of right 12th rib</b>."],"tr":["QL: side-bend, short-looking leg, traction test. Psoas: flexed posture, Thomas test, thigh pain.","Psoas side: L1–L2 rotation, Thomas. Opposite side: piriformis, pelvic shift, leg pain.","In psoas syndrome <b>L1–L2 are flexed</b> but <b>L5 is extended</b>.","Thomas (iliopsoas) vs Thompson (Achilles) vs Spurling (cervical root) vs Phalen (carpal tunnel).","Muscle energy barriers: psoas = hip extension; piriformis = internal rotation; QL = longitudinal stretch, then patient hikes hip.","Stretch photos: straight knee + hip flexed = hamstrings; lunge with hip extended = psoas; heel to buttock = quadriceps.","Pectoralis trigger point → supraventricular tachycardia; flattened thoracic kyphosis → mitral valve prolapse."]},"OMM-W14":{"bp":"Week 14 turns lumbar anatomy into diagnosis. Match the story and exam to the lesion: a disc (root signs, worse with flexion), stenosis (worse standing, better bending forward), the spondylo- family, or an emergency (cauda equina, infection). The lab adds Type II muscle energy (FDR and SUE) and lumbar counterstrain.","sec":[{"h":"Disc herniation and the X+1 rule","html":"<p>The <b>nucleus pulposus</b> sits slightly back in the disc, and the posterior longitudinal ligament (PLL) is only half as wide at L4–L5 as at L1. So discs herniate <b>posterolaterally</b>, and 98% of herniations are at <b>L4–L5 or L5–S1</b>. A bulging disc sags without a tear. A herniated disc (herniated nucleus pulposus, HNP) leaks nucleus through a torn annulus.</p><p>Lumbar roots exit through the foramen <b>below their own vertebra</b>, just above the disc. By the time a disc can touch it, that level's root has already left. So a posterolateral herniation hits the next root down: the <b>X+1 rule</b>.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Lesion</th><th>Root hit</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Posterolateral disc</td><td>X+1 (the root crossing the disc)</td><td>L4–L5 disc → L5; L5–S1 disc → S1</td></tr><tr><td>Foraminal stenosis or far-lateral disc</td><td>X (the exiting root)</td><td>L3–L4 foramen → L3</td></tr><tr><td>Large central disc</td><td>Many roots, both sides</td><td>Cauda equina syndrome</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Squish and squirt</b>: flexion squeezes the front of the disc and pushes the nucleus backward onto the roots. So disc pain gets worse with <b>flexion and sitting</b>, coughing and straining. Extension pushes the nucleus forward. MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) is the gold standard. Treat conservatively: bed rest 2 days at most, OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment) indirect first, then gentle direct; <b>HVLA (high-velocity low-amplitude) is relatively contraindicated</b>. Surgery only if conservative care fails.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O079-60.jpg","cap":"X+1 rule: ruptured L4–L5 disc compresses L5; L4 already exited","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 60"}]},{"h":"Reading the root: L4, L5, S1","html":"<p>Each root has a muscle, a reflex and a skin area. Test them sitting. Then screen with heel and toe walking.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Root</th><th>Motor (screen)</th><th>Reflex</th><th>Sensory</th><th>Disc that hits it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>L4</td><td>Quadriceps (squat and rise); tibialis anterior: dorsiflexion and inversion (heel-walk, foot inverted)</td><td><b>Patellar</b></td><td><b>Medial</b> leg, ankle and foot</td><td>L3–L4</td></tr><tr><td>L5</td><td>Extensor hallucis longus: <b>great-toe extension</b> (heel walk)</td><td>None reliable</td><td>Lateral leg and <b>dorsum of foot</b></td><td>L4–L5</td></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>Calf: <b>plantar flexion</b> (toe walk); peroneus longus: eversion</td><td><b>Achilles</b></td><td>Posterolateral calf, <b>lateral foot and sole</b></td><td>L5–S1</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Interactive-deck slide 63 lists S1 sensation as \"lateral leg and dorsum of foot\". That is L5. S1 is the lateral foot and sole, as the dermatome slide and the lab say. L3 covers the front of the thigh to the medial knee and helps the patellar reflex.</p><p>How <b>root entrapment</b> looks to an osteopath: limited trunk flexion, a protective functional scoliosis, weakness and altered sensation in one dermatome, and a positive straight leg raise with a dural sign. Add a restricted hip drop and tenderness in the <b>multifidus triangle</b> (L4, iliac crest, sacral tip).</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O079-117.jpg","cap":"L4, L5, S1: pain, numbness, weakness, screening walk, reflexes","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 117"}]},{"h":"Special tests","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>How</th><th>Positive means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Straight leg raise</b> (SLR, Lasègue)</td><td>Supine; lift the straight leg</td><td>Pain shooting below the knee before about 70–80° = sciatic root (L4–S3) irritation. Hamstring pain stays in the back of the thigh.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Bragard</b></td><td>Lower the leg 5° from the painful point, then dorsiflex the foot</td><td>Pain returns = nerve root (dural) sign; confirms the SLR</td></tr><tr><td><b>Well-leg (crossed) SLR</b></td><td>Lift the unaffected leg</td><td>Pain in the affected leg = large or central herniation</td></tr><tr><td>Milgram; Valsalva</td><td>Hold both legs 2–3 inches up for 30 seconds; bear down</td><td>Pain = higher intrathecal pressure on a space-occupying lesion (disc, tumor)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Kemp</b></td><td>Extension plus rotation and sidebending to the painful side, 30 seconds</td><td>Back, buttock and thigh pain = stenosis</td></tr><tr><td>Kernig</td><td>Hip and knee at 90°, then straighten the knee</td><td>Pain or hamstring spasm = meningeal irritation</td></tr><tr><td>Babinski</td><td>Stroke the sole from heel to great toe</td><td>Great toe goes up = upper motor neuron lesion</td></tr><tr><td>Spinous process percussion</td><td>Tap with the reflex hammer</td><td>Sharp pain = fracture or infection</td></tr><tr><td>Rectal exam</td><td>Perianal sensation and sphincter tone</td><td>Loss = cauda equina</td></tr></tbody></table>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O079-76.jpg","cap":"Straight leg raise, then lower 5° and dorsiflex the foot","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 76"}]},{"h":"Red flags and emergencies","html":"<p>Most low back pain is mechanical. Look for <b>red flags</b>: onset under 20 or over 55; major trauma; fever, chills or feeling unwell. Others: a history of cancer, HIV, steroids or <b>IV (intravenous) drug use</b>; pain at night or unrelated to activity; thoracic pain; weight loss; widespread neurologic signs; no improvement after <b>4–6 weeks</b>. Order MRI at once for suspected infection, cauda equina or a severe deficit.</p><p><b>Vertebral osteomyelitis with discitis</b> is blood-borne infection of the vertebral body and disc. Classic case: an IV drug user with 8 weeks of back pain that pain medicine does not touch. Contrast MRI shows eroded endplates and an enhancing disc. Worldwide the commonest cause is tuberculosis (Pott's disease), which collapses vertebrae into a <b>gibbus</b> (sharp kyphosis).</p><p><b>Cauda equina syndrome</b>: a large central disc, stenosis, tumor (lung or breast metastases), infection or trauma squeezes the roots inside the <b>thecal (dural) sac</b>. These roots lie below the cord's end at L1–L2. The signs are lower motor neuron: <b>saddle anesthesia</b>, urinary retention with overflow, fecal incontinence and <b>low rectal tone</b>. Add bilateral sciatica, lost knee and ankle reflexes, and rising leg weakness. Order emergency MRI and surgical decompression <b>within 48 hours</b>. A cord lesion above L1 gives upper motor neuron signs instead: brisk reflexes and a Babinski sign.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O079-34.jpg","cap":"Red flags for spinal pathology in low back pain","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 34"}]},{"h":"Degeneration: osteoarthritis, spondylosis, stenosis","html":"<p><b>Lumbar osteoarthritis</b> (OA) starts with disc degeneration at L4–L5 and L5–S1, then facet wear and spurs. On exam, <b>active and passive ROM (range of motion) are restricted in all planes</b>. There is <b>loss of spring</b> with rigid, abrupt end points, and the spinous processes feel closer together. <b>Spondylosis</b> is the imaging name: disc space narrowing, endplate sclerosis, osteophytes (anterior lipping) and ankylosis. Do not confuse it with <b>spondylitis</b>, an inflammatory group (ankylosing, psoriatic, enteropathic, reactive).</p><p><b>Spinal stenosis</b> is a narrowed canal or foramen, usually after 60. Causes: thick facets and ligamentum flavum, a buckled PLL, a bulging disc, osteophytes, tumor or a slip. Standing and walking hold the spine in <b>extension</b>, which narrows the space. That gives <b>neurogenic claudication</b>: back, buttock and thigh pain with heavy, weak legs. Sitting or leaning on a shopping cart (flexion) relieves it. Confirm with MRI.</p><p>Treatment is <b>flexion-biased exercise</b>: supine pelvic tilt, and active resistive flexion (knees to chest, pushing the knees into the hands). Add OMT for motion and blood flow, then NSAIDs (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs), an epidural steroid and finally laminectomy. Extension-inducing (prone) exercise is for the opposite problem, a lost lordosis.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O079-95.jpg","cap":"Normal vs stenotic canal; osteophytes and narrowed disc narrow it","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 95"}]},{"h":"The spondylo- family","html":"<p>Memory hook: <b>lysis</b> = break, <b>listhesis</b> = slip, <b>osis</b> = old (degeneration), <b>itis</b> = inflammation.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>What it is</th><th>Clues</th><th>Imaging</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Spondylolysis</td><td>Pars interarticularis fracture, <b>no slip</b></td><td>Young athlete in extension sports; back pain, tight hamstrings; 80% bilateral, mostly L5</td><td><b>Oblique</b> x-ray: collar on the Scottie dog's neck</td></tr><tr><td>Spondylolisthesis</td><td><b>Forward slip</b> of a body on the one below</td><td>Step-off, hamstring spasm, more lordosis, flat gluteal creases, stiff-legged waddling gait</td><td><b>Lateral</b> x-ray; grade I–IV (0–25, 25–50, 50–75, over 75%)</td></tr><tr><td>Spondylosis</td><td>Degeneration with ankylosis</td><td>Constant back and buttock pain and stiffness</td><td>Disc narrowing, sclerosis, osteophytes</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Scottie dog on the oblique view: nose = transverse process, eye = pedicle, ear = superior articular process, front leg = inferior articular process, <b>neck = pars</b>.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Isthmic spondylolisthesis</th><th>Degenerative spondylolisthesis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cause</td><td>Bilateral pars defects (spondylolysis)</td><td>Facet OA, disc degeneration, thick ligamentum flavum</td></tr><tr><td>Age</td><td>Under 50; commonest cause of back pain and sciatica in children</td><td>Over 50</td></tr><tr><td>Level</td><td>L5–S1</td><td>L4–L5</td></tr><tr><td>Note</td><td>Posterior arch stays behind</td><td>More cauda equina and root compression</td></tr></tbody></table><p>On palpation you feel bogginess and a <b>shelf (step-off)</b> that is still there in flexion. In an isthmic L5–S1 slip the S1 facets hold the L5 arch back. The L5 body slides forward with L4 on top of it, so the step is between the <b>L4 and L5</b> spinous processes. Pain gets worse with extension (one DynaMed slide says the opposite; the rest of the course says worse).</p><p>Treatment: 85–90% recover with conservative care. Treat the dysfunction above the slip and the sacrum. <b>HVLA is relatively contraindicated</b>. Reduce lordosis with pelvic tilt, lose weight, avoid high heels. The review package says \"avoid flexion-based exercise\"; follow the lecture's pelvic tilt.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O079-103.jpg","cap":"Normal pars, spondylolysis break, and spondylolisthesis forward slip","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 103"},{"src":"sl/O079-112.jpg","cap":"Scottie dog outlined on CT and oblique x-ray; arrows at the pars","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 112"}]},{"h":"Congenital variants","html":"<p><b>Spina bifida</b> means the laminae fail to close, usually in the lumbar spine. In <b>occulta</b> nothing herniates; often the only sign is a hair tuft (<b>faun tail nevus</b>), which can also flag a tethered cord. In a <b>meningocele</b> only the meninges herniate. In a <b>meningomyelocele</b> the meninges <b>and neural tissue</b> herniate, with neurologic deficits.</p>","figs":[]},{"h":"Lab: lumbar muscle energy and counterstrain","html":"<p>Type II (non-neutral) dysfunctions are named FRS (flexed, rotated and sidebent to the same side) or ERS (extended, rotated and sidebent). Rotation is toward the <b>posterior transverse process</b> (TP). Lumbar muscle energy (ME) is done side-lying, and two mnemonics give the setup:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Mnemonic</th><th>Dysfunction</th><th>Position</th><th>Posterior TP</th><th>Spine moved into</th><th>Legs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>FDR</b></td><td>Flexed (FRS): extension restricted</td><td>Lateral <b>R</b>ecumbent</td><td><b>D</b>own</td><td>Extension</td><td>Lifted toward the ceiling to sidebend into the barrier</td></tr><tr><td><b>SUE</b></td><td><b>E</b>xtended (ERS): flexion restricted</td><td><b>S</b>ims (chest rolled toward the table)</td><td><b>U</b>p</td><td>Flexion; the Sims roll adds rotation</td><td>Dropped off the table toward the floor</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The patient then pushes the legs back against equal resistance for 3–5 seconds: toward the floor in FDR, toward the ceiling in SUE. Repeat 3–5 times at each new barrier, then retest. For a Type I group curve, the patient lies convex side up and you lift the ankles toward the ceiling. Lumbar ME is relatively contraindicated in an undiagnosed radiculopathy.</p><p><b>Counterstrain</b> (Jones): find the tender point and fold the body over it into a position of ease that cuts tenderness by at least 70%. Hold <b>90 seconds</b>, return slowly and passively, and retest. The <b>anterior L1</b> point is just <b>medial to the ASIS</b> (anterior superior iliac spine); treat it supine with the hips and knees flexed. <b>Posterior</b> points sit on the L1–L5 spinous or transverse processes; treat them prone by lifting the leg into extension, adding rotation for a transverse-process point. Avoid it if the patient cannot relax, the position causes neurologic symptoms, or the point overlies a fracture.</p>","figs":[{"src":"sl/O081-9.jpg","cap":"Anterior L1 tender point medial to the ASIS; supine flexion treatment","where":"2026 week 14 Lab Clinical Lumbar, slide 9"}]},{"h":"Side by side: lumbar pathologies","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Pathology</th><th>Key findings</th><th>Worse / better</th><th>Test</th><th>Imaging sign</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Disc herniation</td><td>One-sided dermatomal leg pain beyond back pain; root weakness, reflex loss; age 30s–40s</td><td>Worse flexion, sitting, coughing; better extension, lying</td><td>SLR with Bragard; well-leg SLR; Milgram, Valsalva</td><td>MRI: nucleus through annulus, posterolateral, L4–L5 or L5–S1</td></tr><tr><td>Spinal stenosis</td><td>Over 60; bilateral buttock and leg pain, heavy legs (neurogenic claudication)</td><td>Worse standing, walking, extension; better sitting, flexion</td><td>Kemp</td><td>MRI: narrow canal or foramina, osteophytes, thick ligamentum flavum</td></tr><tr><td>OA / spondylosis</td><td>Constant stiffness; ROM limited in all planes; loss of spring</td><td>Stiff in every direction; stenosis pattern if severe</td><td>Spring test; active and passive ROM</td><td>Disc narrowing, endplate sclerosis, anterior lipping</td></tr><tr><td>Spondylolysis</td><td>Young athlete; back pain, tight hamstrings</td><td>Worse extension</td><td>Oblique x-ray</td><td>Collar on the Scottie dog's neck</td></tr><tr><td>Spondylolisthesis</td><td>Step-off that stays in flexion; hamstring spasm; usually no neuro deficit</td><td>Worse extension; better flexion</td><td>Palpate the spinous processes</td><td>Lateral x-ray: forward slip, grade I–IV</td></tr><tr><td>Cauda equina</td><td>Saddle anesthesia, retention, low rectal tone, absent knee and ankle reflexes</td><td>Progressive; surgical emergency</td><td>Rectal exam</td><td>Emergency MRI: thecal sac compressed</td></tr><tr><td>Osteomyelitis / discitis</td><td>IV drug use, fever, night pain, more than 4–6 weeks</td><td>Constant; not eased by rest</td><td>Percussion tenderness; ESR (erythrocyte sedimentation rate)</td><td>Contrast MRI: eroded endplates, enhancing disc</td></tr></tbody></table>","figs":[]}],"mk":["<b>X+1 rule</b>: a posterolateral disc at L4–L5 hits L5; at L5–S1 it hits S1.","Roots exit <b>below their own vertebra</b>: foraminal stenosis between L3 and L4 hits L3.","<b>L4</b>: patellar reflex, medial leg, quadriceps and tibialis anterior.","<b>L5</b>: great-toe extension (heel walk), dorsum of foot, no reliable reflex.","<b>S1</b>: Achilles reflex, lateral foot and sole, plantar flexion and eversion (toe walk).","<b>SLR + Bragard</b> dorsiflexion = root irritation; a positive well-leg SLR = large herniation.","Disc pain worse with <b>flexion</b>; stenosis worse with <b>extension</b> (standing, walking), relieved by flexion.","<b>Cauda equina</b> = roots compressed in the thecal sac: saddle anesthesia, retention, low rectal tone, lost reflexes; decompress within 48 hours.","Back pain in an <b>IV drug user</b> that fails treatment = osteomyelitis/discitis; get contrast MRI.","<b>Spondylolysis</b> = pars fracture, no slip; collar on the Scottie dog on the oblique x-ray.","<b>Spondylolisthesis</b> = forward slip, L5 on S1; step-off, hamstring spasm; HVLA relatively contraindicated; reduce lordosis.","<b>Spondylosis</b> = degeneration: disc narrowing, endplate sclerosis, osteophytes.","OA: ROM limited in <b>all planes</b> with <b>loss of spring</b>; stenosis care is flexion (pelvic tilt).","<b>Meningomyelocele</b> = meninges plus neural tissue; meningocele = meninges only; occulta = hair tuft.","<b>FDR</b>: flexed, posterior TP down, lateral recumbent. <b>SUE</b>: Sims, posterior TP up, extended."],"tr":["Spondylo<b>lysis</b> (break, oblique view) vs spondylo<b>listhesis</b> (slip, lateral view) vs spondyl<b>osis</b> (degeneration) vs spondyl<b>itis</b> (inflammation).","Disc herniation (worse with flexion, sitting) vs stenosis (worse with extension, standing).","Neurogenic claudication (needs sitting or flexion) vs vascular claudication (eases on standing still, weak pulses).","Cauda equina (lower motor neuron: absent reflexes) vs cord lesion above L1 (upper motor neuron: brisk reflexes, Babinski).","Posterolateral disc hits root X+1; foraminal stenosis or far-lateral disc hits root X.","L5 = dorsum of foot, no reflex; S1 = lateral foot and sole, Achilles (slide 63 wrongly gives S1 the L5 area).","Isthmic slip at L5–S1: step-off between L4 and L5, because the broken L5 arch stays back.","FDR (posterior TP down, legs lifted) vs SUE (posterior TP up, Sims, legs dropped)."]}}};